WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.720
Hello, everyone.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:12.720
And welcome to
lecture 8 of CME 296.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.620
So today is a special
day because as you know,

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.200
today is the last lecture
of this entire class.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.560
So the menu for today will
be a little bit different.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:28.400
What we'll do is we'll divide
this lecture into two parts.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.640
In the first part, we'll
try to piece together

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.920
everything we've seen in
the class up until now

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.880
and see what we can
take away from it.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.000
And in the second part,
what we'll look at

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:49.880
is adjacent fields where we can
apply what we've have learned.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:52.600
Does that sound good to you?

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.840
So with that, what
we'll do is we'll

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.600
start with the first part,
which is just piecing together

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.390
everything we've
seen this quarter.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:06.310
And the whole goal
of this class has

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:10.430
been to learn how
to generate images.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:14.070
So, for instance, given an
input prompt, how can we

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:18.790
generate an image
that is quite aligned

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:21.630
with the prompt as input.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.430
And of course,
there is just a lot

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.550
of dimensions we can look at.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:31.110
So this class has
been about decomposing

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:34.790
the fact of learning how it
works into tractable parts.

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:38.550
And if you remember,
the first three lectures

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:43.010
were about just understanding
how we could generate images.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:46.510
Just let's suppose we
have a black box model.

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:48.990
What is the paradigm
that would allow

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:53.150
us to just generate images.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:59.270
So the first lecture was
about diffusion, just

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:03.150
learning how we could do that
using the diffusion paradigm.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:09.350
And if you remember the
first thing we said was,

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:13.230
what could be a good
starting point for us

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.430
to generate those images?

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:18.550
So if you think
about it, the images

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:20.750
that we want to
generate they are

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:24.630
a part of a data distribution
that we do not know.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:28.510
And that data distribution
can be complex,

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:31.270
and it's difficult
to sample from it.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:35.030
So one thing we said was,
what are some distributions

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:38.230
that we know how to sample from?

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:43.230
So one such distribution was
the Gaussian distribution.

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:47.790
And what we said was
maybe in order for us

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:51.710
to sample from this
complicated data distribution,

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.510
one thing we could
do could be to sample

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:58.140
from an easy
distribution and then

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:03.620
make our way up to the
complicated data distribution.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.020
So in this first
lecture, what we

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:09.220
said was let's represent
images with just

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:14.500
a multi-dimension variables
that we could denote x.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:19.060
And what we said
was in order for us

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:24.380
to learn how to go
from Gaussian noise

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.580
to clean images,
what we'll do is

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.940
we will come up with
a forward process

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:36.620
that we define that will allow
us to corrupt clean images

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:38.660
into noise.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:42.620
And the purpose of
diffusion is to learn

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:45.500
how to reverse that process.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:47.660
So if you remember,
the forward process

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:52.860
was a nice formulation
that involves also

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.770
Gaussian distributions.

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:59.370
And so in order for
us to derive the form

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:04.130
of that reverse
process, what we did was

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:10.050
we tried to maximize the
likelihood of seeing the data

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.530
distribution under p theta.

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:15.570
And we went through
several steps that

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:17.930
allowed us to derive a loss.

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:23.410
So if you remember what we said
was maximizing the likelihood

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:24.950
is actually not tractable.

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:29.930
So what we do is we derive
a tractable lower bound,

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:35.490
which was something where we
used the forward process that we

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.970
defined in it.

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:41.130
So this lower bound
is called ELBO.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:44.610
And what we did is
we expanded the terms

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.090
within that lower bound.

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:50.830
And we showed that we
could actually compute it.

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:53.600
It was actually tractable.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.600
And we ended up
with a loss on us

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:02.960
trying to estimate the noise
that was added to a given image

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.400
through this very
simple L2 regression

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:12.480
loss, which had a noised image
as input along with its noise

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:13.680
level.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:17.120
And what it was trying
to do was to estimate

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:20.280
the amount of noise that
was added to the image

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:24.160
or to the noisy
image, I should say.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:28.360
So this was a first way
of looking at things.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:33.320
So diffusion allowed us to
go from an easy to sample

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:36.560
from distribution, so
the Gaussian distribution

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.880
to the data
distribution by learning

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:43.520
how to remove the noise.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:47.080
The second lecture
was about another way

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:55.080
of doing things, which was let's
look at this data distribution,

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:58.260
not from noise that
you need to remove,

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:03.000
but more in terms of
where you need to go.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:05.280
And we looked at
a quantity called

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:11.800
the score, which was defined as
the gradient of the log of p.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:15.040
And we saw that that
quantity had nice properties.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:19.880
And in particular, it
allowed us to not worry

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:24.120
about that normalizing
constant that is intractable.

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:26.520
And what the score
was telling us

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:32.140
was where to go in order to
land at that data distribution.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:34.880
And in particular,
there was a nice formula

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:38.440
from Langevin dynamics,
which basically

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:42.760
said that if you knew
how to compute the score,

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:47.720
then what you could do
is sample from noise

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:52.830
and make your way to
the data distribution.

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:55.030
So the whole goal
of this lecture

00:06:55.030 --> 00:07:00.630
was to derive a way to
estimate that score.

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:05.830
So the problem is the score is
not the quantity that we know.

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:11.550
So what we did was have a
way to introduce the Gaussian

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:15.990
distribution to some
extent so that we

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:19.430
can leverage the properties
that we know from the Gaussian

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:20.550
distribution.

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:22.790
And in particular,
the fact that we

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:27.150
knew how to compute
in an analytical way

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:30.350
the score of a
Gaussian distribution.

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:36.190
So what we did is we took
observation from the target data

00:07:36.190 --> 00:07:37.670
distributions.

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:41.310
And what we did
is we noised them.

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:46.110
And then we looked at how
we could estimate the score

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:49.580
of the noise distribution.

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:53.260
So we saw that it was something
that we could actually compute.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:55.780
But then there was a trade off.

00:07:55.780 --> 00:08:00.940
The more noise you would put
in the data distribution,

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:02.980
the more your noise
data distribution

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:07.980
would be far from the data
distribution of interest.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:11.260
But then when you
do that, you're

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:16.700
able to estimate the score of
the noise data distribution

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:17.940
quite easily.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:20.588
So you have a trade off of
you can compute the quantity,

00:08:20.588 --> 00:08:22.380
but it's not the quantity
that you actually

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:29.660
want versus if you have only
a small amount of noise,

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:33.539
then the score of that low
noise data distribution

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:37.380
is actually not something that
you can estimate very well.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:41.539
But the target that
you want to estimate

00:08:41.539 --> 00:08:45.740
is closer to the actual
quantity that you want.

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:48.460
So you had this trade
off, and what we said

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:55.060
was, well, how about we
estimate the score, not just

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:58.380
as a function of
where we are in space,

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:05.060
but also as a function
of data distribution is.

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:08.780
And we ended up with the
de-noising score matching

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:12.740
loss, which allowed
us to estimate

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:14.540
the score of that
noisy distribution,

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:20.220
given a noisy image
and a noise level.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:23.940
And once we did
that, we actually

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:27.340
saw that this way
of looking at things

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:33.940
was actually very similar to the
way we did in the first lecture

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:35.700
with diffusion.

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:40.460
And we saw that actually the
score of the forward process

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:48.650
is equal to minus the noise that
you added over some coefficient.

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:53.830
So both approaches led
to similar formulations.

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:58.290
And we saw that we
actually had this identity.

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:01.730
And then what we did
was just realized

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:05.890
that the way we were noising
our data distribution

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:10.770
was in a discrete way, and that
there are many choices that you

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:13.170
had to take at the
very beginning,

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:17.370
such as how many steps you
want to take and so on.

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:24.850
And so this motivated us to
move from discrete formulation

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:28.010
to a continuous formulation.

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:31.210
So we derived the
continuous formulation

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:36.010
of the forward process,
which allowed us to come up

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:39.170
with this stochastic
differential equation, which

00:10:39.170 --> 00:10:44.980
is the delta in position
during the forward process,

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:49.640
which is equal to a drift
term that is deterministic,

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:54.920
plus a diffusion term,
which is stochastic.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:58.280
And here we're using the
continuous equivalent

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:04.920
of the noise, which as we saw
was the Wiener process, dW.

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:08.920
And you could
actually figure out

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:13.040
how to move your x when you
want it to noise it with respect

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.240
to these two terms.

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:21.880
And we actually saw
that the first approach

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:25.400
that we looked at in the
first lecture and then

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:28.880
the second approach
that we looked at here

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:34.280
were actually special cases
of this more generic formula.

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:38.630
In particular, the DDPM
formulation from lecture 1

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:42.870
was a variance-preserving
formulation,

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:46.230
whereas the one that we saw
with the noise condition

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:53.350
score networks was actually a
variance exploding formulation.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:57.190
And then what we
did was OK, our goal

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:59.830
is not just to noise images.

00:11:59.830 --> 00:12:03.030
Our goal is to learn
the reverse process.

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:06.510
And so in order to do that,
the nice thing was that

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:12.870
in the '80s, we have a nice
results that told us that

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:18.350
for a given forward process,
you could have a corresponding

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:21.550
reverse process where you
actually needed to know

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:25.190
the score, which is actually the
quantity that we're estimating

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:28.110
here.

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:31.590
So what we had to do was just
to estimate the score in order

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:34.750
to reverse the process.

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:37.030
So long story
short, lecture 1 we

00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:40.790
saw in order to go
from noise to clean,

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:44.470
we need to predict
the noise to remove.

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:47.990
In lecture 2, we said,
in order to go from noise

00:12:47.990 --> 00:12:53.230
to clean, we need to know
where we want to move towards,

00:12:53.230 --> 00:12:54.910
which is the score.

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:57.350
You can think of the
score as a compass

00:12:57.350 --> 00:13:02.990
that tells you where the
data distribution is located.

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:06.310
And then we ended up
in the third lecture

00:13:06.310 --> 00:13:10.110
with a third way of
looking at things, which

00:13:10.110 --> 00:13:15.670
is the flow matching
formulation, which actually

00:13:15.670 --> 00:13:18.790
framed the problem
in a different way.

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:23.430
So what we did there was to
consider the noisy distribution

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:27.790
in the beginning as just
an initial distribution,

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:30.230
and the data
distribution of interest

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:33.790
was just a target distribution.

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:37.860
And the goal for us
was to figure out

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:41.500
how to move your
probability density

00:13:41.500 --> 00:13:46.620
from an initial distribution
to a target distribution.

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:49.480
So that's the flow matching
way of looking at things.

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:56.100
It's the point of view
of mass transport.

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:58.180
And in particular,
there was one quantity

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:00.980
that was very
important, which is

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:04.680
called the vector
field or the velocity,

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:07.460
which is denoted Ut of x.

00:14:07.460 --> 00:14:10.740
It is a vector that is
defined at every position

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:12.780
and at all time t.

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:15.340
And what we saw
was that in order

00:14:15.340 --> 00:14:20.700
to move each observations
from one point

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:25.340
to another, what we had
to do was to just follow

00:14:25.340 --> 00:14:26.940
the vector field.

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:31.580
So we had this microscopic way
of looking at things, which

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:33.410
is the ODE that you see here.

00:14:33.410 --> 00:14:37.650
So the dx over dt, which
is equal to the vector

00:14:37.650 --> 00:14:43.210
field at that time
t and at position x.

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:48.590
And we also had a second way
of looking at this same thing.

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:52.190
So the first formulation is
the microscopic formulation,

00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:57.770
which is what is happening
to your individual particles.

00:14:57.770 --> 00:15:01.570
The second way of looking
at this same thing

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:04.690
is at the macro
level, where what

00:15:04.690 --> 00:15:11.410
you say is the evolution of the
probability at a given time t

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:16.410
is equal to minus the
probability flux, which

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:21.570
is what constitutes the
continuity equation, which just

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:25.850
tells you that you're not
losing anything between time t

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:28.730
equals 0 and 1.

00:15:28.730 --> 00:15:33.370
And the whole point of us
going into these formulas

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:40.250
was for us to figure
out a vector field which

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:44.530
would allow us to go from
this initial distribution

00:15:44.530 --> 00:15:47.370
to this target
data distribution.

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:50.010
So this was the whole point.

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:55.970
And in particular, let's assume
you know the vector field

00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:58.730
that you want to estimate.

00:15:58.730 --> 00:16:03.090
The strategy here would
be to have a model that

00:16:03.090 --> 00:16:05.030
estimates the vector field.

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:08.850
So here, Ut theta of x.

00:16:08.850 --> 00:16:12.290
So let's assume you
learn such a model.

00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:14.610
If you do learn
such a model, then

00:16:14.610 --> 00:16:19.490
in order to sample from
the complicated data

00:16:19.490 --> 00:16:23.050
distribution which is
p1, what you need to do

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:25.770
is to just sample
from your easy data

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:30.000
distribution p0 and
then numerically

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:38.440
solve the [INAUDIBLE] here
between times 0 [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:44.320
using the vector field
that you have learned.

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:48.280
And we saw that it's
actually something

00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:53.600
that you can do in practice if
you construct the target vector

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:56.500
field in a way that
makes it tractable.

00:16:56.500 --> 00:17:01.400
So we saw conditional
probability paths,

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:04.839
which were an easier
way of looking

00:17:04.839 --> 00:17:09.280
at this, which is we
don't know what the vector

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:11.319
field in the general case.

00:17:11.319 --> 00:17:14.880
How about if we look
at a simpler case where

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:19.880
we want to move the initial data
distribution, not to the target

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:23.839
data distribution,
but to a single point,

00:17:23.839 --> 00:17:28.790
which was the conditional
probability path that we saw?

00:17:28.790 --> 00:17:34.950
And we saw that we could obtain
a conditional flow matching

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:39.670
loss that was equivalent to you
learning the vector field that

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:44.350
was the aggregate of all these
conditional vector fields that

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:49.150
allowed you to solve
the problem of interest.

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:52.830
So these first three
lectures were by far the most

00:17:52.830 --> 00:17:54.250
mathematically challenging.

00:17:54.250 --> 00:17:56.510
I hope what I said made sense.

00:17:56.510 --> 00:17:58.850
We spent six hours on this.

00:17:58.850 --> 00:18:02.510
And I hope I did a
good job in 10 minutes

00:18:02.510 --> 00:18:04.590
to just recap what we did.

00:18:04.590 --> 00:18:07.950
At the end of it, I just
want you to remember

00:18:07.950 --> 00:18:10.990
that so we're in 2026.

00:18:10.990 --> 00:18:15.550
People nowadays they use
flow matching by default.

00:18:15.550 --> 00:18:19.150
And so if you just
want to have something

00:18:19.150 --> 00:18:24.170
that you want to really
master perfectly,

00:18:24.170 --> 00:18:27.540
I would recommend just really
understanding the flow matching

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:32.260
part because this is what
most models nowadays use.

00:18:32.260 --> 00:18:34.420
In particular,
they use a variant

00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:39.580
that we saw that's called the
rectified flow variant, which

00:18:39.580 --> 00:18:45.340
was an extension of this
that allowed the path

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:49.300
to be straighter, so
that at inference time,

00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:56.020
you could afford having fewer
steps in the numerical solver

00:18:56.020 --> 00:19:03.220
here so that you can just
sample images with fewer steps.

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:04.420
Oof.

00:19:04.420 --> 00:19:08.280
So first three lectures,
mathematically challenging.

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:14.420
It was purely for us to learn
a paradigm to generate images.

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:20.260
But up until that point,
we assumed two things.

00:19:20.260 --> 00:19:24.380
So the first one is we assumed
that we were generating

00:19:24.380 --> 00:19:27.220
images in an unconditioned way.

00:19:27.220 --> 00:19:29.640
So we didn't have
any input prompts.

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:32.180
We did not worry about this.

00:19:32.180 --> 00:19:36.020
And the second thing was
we just assumed that we

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:38.180
had a way to represent images.

00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:42.460
So we just represented them
with x with, I don't know,

00:19:42.460 --> 00:19:45.780
n dimensional vector.

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:49.820
But we did not see what
representation would make sense.

00:19:49.820 --> 00:19:55.980
And this was the focus of
lecture 4, which was titled

00:19:55.980 --> 00:19:59.460
latent space and guidance.

00:19:59.460 --> 00:20:03.340
And what we saw there
was, well, in order

00:20:03.340 --> 00:20:07.900
to have a meaningful
representation of your images,

00:20:07.900 --> 00:20:14.700
what you want is to have a way
to keep only the useful stuff.

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:16.180
So how do you do that?

00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:18.740
Well, in pixel space,
you see that you

00:20:18.740 --> 00:20:23.490
have a pretty large amount
of spatial correlation, which

00:20:23.490 --> 00:20:26.050
means that if you
look at a pixel,

00:20:26.050 --> 00:20:30.730
a lot of the surrounding pixels
are more or less of that value.

00:20:30.730 --> 00:20:34.210
So you can imagine that there is
a lot of redundant information

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:34.710
in there.

00:20:34.710 --> 00:20:37.010
So that's number 1.

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:43.090
And number 2 is in
the pixel space, well,

00:20:43.090 --> 00:20:46.410
the dimensionality
is pretty high.

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:50.490
So given that the model that
you're going to build from it

00:20:50.490 --> 00:20:55.050
is going to be a function of the
dimension of your input, what

00:20:55.050 --> 00:21:00.730
you want to do is to make sure
your input is not too big.

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:03.490
So you want to
compress the input

00:21:03.490 --> 00:21:09.370
into something that is only
keeping you the useful bits.

00:21:09.370 --> 00:21:13.090
And in order for
us to do that, we

00:21:13.090 --> 00:21:18.290
looked at a model that was
called an autoencoder, whose

00:21:18.290 --> 00:21:27.320
only goal was to learn a way to
represent images into a smaller

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:28.400
space.

00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:31.160
That's called the latent space.

00:21:31.160 --> 00:21:35.320
So here on the slide, you
see that you have the image

00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:37.160
in the pixel space as inputs.

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:40.400
And then you have an encoder
that does some downsampling

00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:45.280
operations that gets you some
representation of the input

00:21:45.280 --> 00:21:49.560
image, not in the pixel space,
but in a lower dimensional

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:52.800
space, which is
the latent space.

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:57.960
And what you do here is to
use that lower dimensional

00:21:57.960 --> 00:22:04.680
representation in order
to reconstruct your input.

00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:09.000
So you have a proxy task,
which is how can you

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:13.400
reconstruct the input
as best as you can

00:22:13.400 --> 00:22:16.200
by going through
this bottleneck.

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:18.720
And when you do
that, you are indeed

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:23.640
able to learn a latent space
that compresses the information

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:26.520
into just fewer dimensions.

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:29.040
But then the problem
that we encountered

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:34.760
was that if you did that,
you had no way of controlling

00:22:34.760 --> 00:22:38.480
the shape of the latent space.

00:22:38.480 --> 00:22:41.800
And in particular,
if you want to learn

00:22:41.800 --> 00:22:45.120
how to generate
images, what you want

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:48.300
is to have an easy
time at learning it.

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:52.920
So you don't want a space
with spikes here and there

00:22:52.920 --> 00:22:57.200
and then nothing in between.

00:22:57.200 --> 00:22:59.160
So what you want
is something more

00:22:59.160 --> 00:23:04.960
at the top of the slide, which
is a data distribution that

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:09.400
is more compact and
well put together.

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:11.840
So in order for us
to do that, what

00:23:11.840 --> 00:23:16.870
we did was go back to the
way we formulated things

00:23:16.870 --> 00:23:23.950
and add a constraint for us to
regularize the latent space.

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:27.290
And this was the
VAE that we saw--

00:23:27.290 --> 00:23:29.950
the variational autoencoder--

00:23:29.950 --> 00:23:34.630
which allowed us to structure
the latent space in a way that

00:23:34.630 --> 00:23:38.710
would make our lives easier.

00:23:38.710 --> 00:23:42.990
And we saw that in order
to derive a loss for us

00:23:42.990 --> 00:23:48.750
to learn that model, we use the
same trick as lecture 1, which

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:50.270
leverages the ELBO.

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:51.990
So the evidence lower bound.

00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:53.850
I'm not going to
go into details.

00:23:53.850 --> 00:23:55.990
But one thing I
wanted to say here

00:23:55.990 --> 00:24:01.590
is we ended up with a
loss that had two terms.

00:24:01.590 --> 00:24:06.310
One was the pixel-wise
reconstruction loss,

00:24:06.310 --> 00:24:09.670
which we had
previously, but also

00:24:09.670 --> 00:24:13.910
a second term that was aiming
at structuring the latent

00:24:13.910 --> 00:24:18.420
space as much as possible to
a certain distribution that

00:24:18.420 --> 00:24:21.140
was called the prior
distribution, p of z,

00:24:21.140 --> 00:24:25.100
so that we can have something
that looks a little bit more

00:24:25.100 --> 00:24:27.040
like the top one.

00:24:29.740 --> 00:24:30.240
Cool.

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:33.000
So that was really the
focus of that session.

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:35.760
And we also learned how
to represent the input.

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:40.460
So we saw different encoders,
in particular transformer-based

00:24:40.460 --> 00:24:44.620
encoders, including the
VIT-- vision transformer.

00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:46.380
And then we saw
that there are ways

00:24:46.380 --> 00:24:50.740
to combine different
modalities in the same space,

00:24:50.740 --> 00:24:55.460
for instance, with CLIP
using a contrastive loss.

00:24:55.460 --> 00:25:01.780
And then we also saw methods
to incentivize our generation

00:25:01.780 --> 00:25:04.440
to be more aligned
with our condition.

00:25:04.440 --> 00:25:05.940
And here, if you
remember, we looked

00:25:05.940 --> 00:25:11.700
at classifier-free guidance
that allowed us to do that.

00:25:11.700 --> 00:25:12.280
Cool.

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:17.290
So up until that
point, we knew how

00:25:17.290 --> 00:25:19.970
to generate images in
terms of the way we

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:21.830
wanted to go about doing this.

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:24.530
We knew how to
represent the inputs.

00:25:24.530 --> 00:25:28.370
And we knew how to
represent our noisy latents.

00:25:28.370 --> 00:25:32.250
Well, now the question is, what
model are you actually going

00:25:32.250 --> 00:25:34.610
to use to do that?

00:25:34.610 --> 00:25:38.970
And this was lecture 5,
which was on image generation

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:41.850
architectures.

00:25:41.850 --> 00:25:48.450
And here we took a step back
and just reminded ourselves

00:25:48.450 --> 00:25:53.490
of the problem that we have at
hand, which is we have a model.

00:25:53.490 --> 00:25:57.650
And what we want
to do is to predict

00:25:57.650 --> 00:26:03.730
a quantity that will allow
us to go from noise to clean.

00:26:03.730 --> 00:26:08.010
So as I mentioned, the paradigm
that is by far the most common

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:10.230
these days is the flow
matching paradigm.

00:26:10.230 --> 00:26:15.690
So it's, in particular, a model
that would predict the velocity.

00:26:15.690 --> 00:26:18.810
So what we want is
a generation model

00:26:18.810 --> 00:26:24.970
that takes in a noise level,
the condition of interest,

00:26:24.970 --> 00:26:30.730
like your user prompt, and then
your noisy latent as inputs

00:26:30.730 --> 00:26:35.090
in order to predict
the velocity.

00:26:35.090 --> 00:26:38.930
And we saw that one
popular architecture

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:45.750
was the U-Net, which was
composed of two main parts.

00:26:45.750 --> 00:26:49.050
The first one was a
downsampling part,

00:26:49.050 --> 00:26:53.970
which allowed to gain a global
understanding of the inputs

00:26:53.970 --> 00:26:57.290
through downsampling
operations, which

00:26:57.290 --> 00:27:00.970
allowed its receptive field
to be bigger and bigger.

00:27:00.970 --> 00:27:05.810
So here what we mean is when
you compute the activation map,

00:27:05.810 --> 00:27:08.970
if you take a single
value, what we're saying

00:27:08.970 --> 00:27:15.320
is that that value was a
result of a pretty wide input.

00:27:15.320 --> 00:27:16.780
So that's the downsampling part.

00:27:16.780 --> 00:27:18.680
And then the upsampling
part was just a way

00:27:18.680 --> 00:27:23.800
for us to match the
shape that we wanted.

00:27:23.800 --> 00:27:27.960
And we had this copy
and crop connections

00:27:27.960 --> 00:27:33.360
that allowed us to transfer
lower level details that

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:39.280
were computed as part of
the downsampling operation.

00:27:39.280 --> 00:27:40.460
So this was the U-Net.

00:27:40.460 --> 00:27:46.040
But of course, you know that
in 2017, transformers just

00:27:46.040 --> 00:27:48.520
took the whole field by storm.

00:27:48.520 --> 00:27:51.960
And there was more
and more models

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:53.560
that were relying
on the transformer

00:27:53.560 --> 00:27:56.000
and the self-attention
mechanism.

00:27:56.000 --> 00:28:01.240
And it was not long after
that the diffusion transformer

00:28:01.240 --> 00:28:02.440
came into play.

00:28:02.440 --> 00:28:05.560
So in 2022, I believe.

00:28:05.560 --> 00:28:10.190
So that architecture dealt with
a limitation of the U-Nets,

00:28:10.190 --> 00:28:15.910
which was that patches
that were far from one

00:28:15.910 --> 00:28:18.750
another from the
image could not have

00:28:18.750 --> 00:28:22.350
a direct way of interacting
with one another.

00:28:22.350 --> 00:28:26.230
And in cases, like if you
wanted to generate an image,

00:28:26.230 --> 00:28:29.350
let's say, a teddy bear that
looks at himself in the mirror,

00:28:29.350 --> 00:28:32.990
then you would want to have
that interaction to figure out

00:28:32.990 --> 00:28:35.750
the lower level details.

00:28:35.750 --> 00:28:38.830
And we saw that
diffusion transformer

00:28:38.830 --> 00:28:45.630
was injecting conditions
using an adaptive layer norm

00:28:45.630 --> 00:28:46.490
framework.

00:28:46.490 --> 00:28:53.070
So it was just modulating the
embeddings of the patches.

00:28:53.070 --> 00:28:54.830
And then we saw that--

00:28:54.830 --> 00:28:58.330
today is May 29th 2026.

00:28:58.330 --> 00:29:02.890
So now we have a lot of
such models out there.

00:29:02.890 --> 00:29:05.860
And in particular, there
is one specific category

00:29:05.860 --> 00:29:09.980
that has emerged, which is
the multi-modal diffusion

00:29:09.980 --> 00:29:12.900
transformer, which
on top of doing

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:16.540
this self-attention
mechanism, also considers

00:29:16.540 --> 00:29:22.180
the condition as part
of the joint attention.

00:29:22.180 --> 00:29:23.800
And we looked at the timeline.

00:29:23.800 --> 00:29:26.420
So here what's in blue is U-Net.

00:29:26.420 --> 00:29:29.920
And what's in green
is DiT and variants.

00:29:29.920 --> 00:29:32.460
And of course, the
timeline is not exhaustive

00:29:32.460 --> 00:29:37.860
but it is just a way for you
to see that nowadays image

00:29:37.860 --> 00:29:42.460
generation models are
almost everyone is relying

00:29:42.460 --> 00:29:44.200
on the DiT-based architecture.

00:29:46.960 --> 00:29:47.460
Cool.

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:53.480
And then at that stage, we knew
how we could generate images.

00:29:53.480 --> 00:29:55.120
We knew how to
represent the input.

00:29:55.120 --> 00:29:57.080
We knew how to
represent the latentce.

00:29:57.080 --> 00:29:59.140
We knew what model
to use, but we

00:29:59.140 --> 00:30:01.460
didn't how to train that model.

00:30:01.460 --> 00:30:05.620
And that was the
focus of lecture 6.

00:30:05.620 --> 00:30:09.180
And so before digging
into the details,

00:30:09.180 --> 00:30:12.180
one thing we wanted
to just align

00:30:12.180 --> 00:30:15.780
was how we wanted our
time step to be sampled

00:30:15.780 --> 00:30:22.980
during the training, because we
had seen back in lecture 1 to 3

00:30:22.980 --> 00:30:26.220
that the time step that
we were sampling in order

00:30:26.220 --> 00:30:31.780
to train our model was drawn
from a uniform distribution.

00:30:31.780 --> 00:30:36.980
And we spent some time just
thinking about whether all noise

00:30:36.980 --> 00:30:41.300
levels are equally hard or not.

00:30:41.300 --> 00:30:47.120
And we saw that noise levels
that are not super noisy,

00:30:47.120 --> 00:30:51.220
but not towards clean,
like those in the middle,

00:30:51.220 --> 00:30:53.900
were the most
difficult because those

00:30:53.900 --> 00:30:57.860
were the places where we had
to make important decisions

00:30:57.860 --> 00:30:59.540
about where to go.

00:30:59.540 --> 00:31:02.770
And so that's the
reason why the fact

00:31:02.770 --> 00:31:07.650
of sampling from a
uniform distribution

00:31:07.650 --> 00:31:09.730
was not the most optimal.

00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:12.090
And we saw that there
was this distribution

00:31:12.090 --> 00:31:17.250
called the logit-normal
distribution, which

00:31:17.250 --> 00:31:21.330
is a distribution that focuses
much more on the middle steps,

00:31:21.330 --> 00:31:26.010
was something that was more
commonly used these days.

00:31:26.010 --> 00:31:32.250
And we saw that not only
that, but the resolution

00:31:32.250 --> 00:31:35.690
of your images also
matter in terms

00:31:35.690 --> 00:31:39.250
of what the perceived noise is.

00:31:39.250 --> 00:31:42.650
And in particular, if
you want to train a model

00:31:42.650 --> 00:31:45.690
on higher resolution
images, what

00:31:45.690 --> 00:31:49.050
you want is to
make sure to noise

00:31:49.050 --> 00:31:53.050
your images a bit more than
what you did previously, because

00:31:53.050 --> 00:31:58.250
for a given noise level,
a lower resolution image

00:31:58.250 --> 00:32:02.960
will appear as being more noisy
compared to higher resolution

00:32:02.960 --> 00:32:03.800
one.

00:32:03.800 --> 00:32:06.960
And we did some math on the
blackboard, if you remember,

00:32:06.960 --> 00:32:08.960
when we computed
just the uncertainty

00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:12.240
around the underlying
pixel value.

00:32:12.240 --> 00:32:15.160
But intuitively, it
comes from the fact

00:32:15.160 --> 00:32:19.600
that there is spatial
correlation within your image.

00:32:19.600 --> 00:32:23.720
So if you have more
pixels to represent,

00:32:23.720 --> 00:32:29.720
let's say, an area of the image,
well, if you noise some of it,

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:33.000
and if you have more chances
to see the actual value,

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:35.120
then when you look
at the image, you

00:32:35.120 --> 00:32:37.920
will have an easier
time to see what

00:32:37.920 --> 00:32:41.720
the image is about as opposed to
if you have a lower resolution

00:32:41.720 --> 00:32:43.280
image.

00:32:43.280 --> 00:32:46.000
So if you have pixels
that are noised,

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:49.360
then you have fewer
chances of seeing

00:32:49.360 --> 00:32:54.800
the underlying true pixel
value-- denoise pixel value.

00:32:54.800 --> 00:32:57.280
And so with that,
we saw that there

00:32:57.280 --> 00:33:01.480
are a few stages to a
typical model training

00:33:01.480 --> 00:33:05.040
process, the first one
being pre-training,

00:33:05.040 --> 00:33:09.360
which is learning how
to generate images.

00:33:09.360 --> 00:33:13.040
And so here you have-- so
it's the most time consuming

00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:15.780
and expensive part of
the whole pipeline,

00:33:15.780 --> 00:33:21.000
because you need to come up with
a very big corpus of images that

00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:25.040
encompasses all of
the possible images

00:33:25.040 --> 00:33:26.400
that you want to generate.

00:33:26.400 --> 00:33:29.580
And it needs to be
certain quality,

00:33:29.580 --> 00:33:32.540
and you need to have
good mixtures and so on.

00:33:32.540 --> 00:33:40.240
So a lot of effort is put into
having a pre-trained data that

00:33:40.240 --> 00:33:43.560
is reflective of the
way you want your image

00:33:43.560 --> 00:33:46.960
generation to learn.

00:33:46.960 --> 00:33:52.520
And after that, we
saw that once you

00:33:52.520 --> 00:33:56.270
get an image
generation model that

00:33:56.270 --> 00:33:59.390
knows how to generate
images, what you wanted to do

00:33:59.390 --> 00:34:02.550
is to generate good images.

00:34:02.550 --> 00:34:07.750
And in particular, good
here can be something

00:34:07.750 --> 00:34:10.889
that people say just
in the aesthetics term.

00:34:10.889 --> 00:34:13.590
So you want your
image to look good,

00:34:13.590 --> 00:34:16.070
but you also want
your image generation

00:34:16.070 --> 00:34:21.270
model know how to generate
images in your field of interest

00:34:21.270 --> 00:34:24.150
or in your task of interest.

00:34:24.150 --> 00:34:27.870
So, for instance, if you have
an image generation model that

00:34:27.870 --> 00:34:31.030
has been pre-trained
on, let's say, nature

00:34:31.030 --> 00:34:34.310
and then, I don't know,
houses, but if you

00:34:34.310 --> 00:34:38.389
want that model to
generate images, let's say,

00:34:38.389 --> 00:34:41.050
teddy bears, so what you
want is, for instance,

00:34:41.050 --> 00:34:44.469
to have an extension
of that training

00:34:44.469 --> 00:34:50.030
that we call continued training,
where you teach your model how

00:34:50.030 --> 00:34:56.699
to generate images in your set
of images of interest, which

00:34:56.699 --> 00:35:00.060
here can be teddy bears.

00:35:00.060 --> 00:35:05.140
And what we saw was that
a third optional step

00:35:05.140 --> 00:35:10.980
could be the tuning step, where
you could have a situation where

00:35:10.980 --> 00:35:15.520
you want to generate images
of one particular subject,

00:35:15.520 --> 00:35:20.980
of one particular
person many times.

00:35:20.980 --> 00:35:25.420
So if you are in that
situation, what you want

00:35:25.420 --> 00:35:30.500
is to tweak your model in
a way that will remember

00:35:30.500 --> 00:35:33.140
what you wanted to generate.

00:35:33.140 --> 00:35:37.780
And we saw DreamBooth as
a way for us to do that.

00:35:37.780 --> 00:35:42.560
So the idea was to gather
a set of images of 5 to 10

00:35:42.560 --> 00:35:47.980
typically that contains
who you want to depict.

00:35:47.980 --> 00:35:52.610
And what you do is
you train your model

00:35:52.610 --> 00:35:56.570
to generate images that
contain that specific person

00:35:56.570 --> 00:36:04.010
or that specific object, while
having a rare token as input.

00:36:04.010 --> 00:36:05.570
So what you are
teaching your model

00:36:05.570 --> 00:36:10.410
was to rewire its brain
towards generating something

00:36:10.410 --> 00:36:13.770
when it saw a specific token.

00:36:13.770 --> 00:36:18.250
And given that these
models can be pretty big,

00:36:18.250 --> 00:36:21.970
we saw that there
were also techniques

00:36:21.970 --> 00:36:26.570
to tune, not all
weights, but a subset

00:36:26.570 --> 00:36:30.730
of the weights through this
technique called LoRA--

00:36:30.730 --> 00:36:34.490
low-rank adaptation.

00:36:34.490 --> 00:36:37.710
And once you did that--

00:36:37.710 --> 00:36:40.770
let's suppose you did either
the first two or all three

00:36:40.770 --> 00:36:41.670
of the steps.

00:36:41.670 --> 00:36:45.170
What you want is to now deploy
your model to production.

00:36:45.170 --> 00:36:51.370
So what you want is to have a
model that does not cost you

00:36:51.370 --> 00:36:56.310
a lot and also does not take a
lot of time to make generations.

00:36:56.310 --> 00:36:58.890
So we saw a bunch
of methods that

00:36:58.890 --> 00:37:04.090
were aimed at shortening the
number of steps that were needed

00:37:04.090 --> 00:37:06.770
for you to generate samples.

00:37:06.770 --> 00:37:11.370
So this category of methods
is called distillation.

00:37:11.370 --> 00:37:14.050
So we saw a number of methods.

00:37:14.050 --> 00:37:17.950
So I would encourage you
to look at that lecture.

00:37:17.950 --> 00:37:20.850
So there was progressive
distillation as one example,

00:37:20.850 --> 00:37:24.890
but some others as well.

00:37:24.890 --> 00:37:30.270
And then finally, last week,
what we said was great,

00:37:30.270 --> 00:37:32.130
we can do all of this.

00:37:32.130 --> 00:37:36.610
But we have not seen how
we could evaluate how

00:37:36.610 --> 00:37:40.610
our images, how good they were.

00:37:40.610 --> 00:37:42.610
And if you don't
know how to do that,

00:37:42.610 --> 00:37:46.270
well, you don't where
to put your efforts.

00:37:46.270 --> 00:37:48.250
So you need to know
how well you're doing,

00:37:48.250 --> 00:37:55.560
which is why lecture 7 was
focused on evaluating images

00:37:55.560 --> 00:37:58.860
and in particular, how
good those images were.

00:38:01.520 --> 00:38:07.640
So we saw that the most common
way people evaluate images

00:38:07.640 --> 00:38:13.640
on things like leaderboards
is by having images generated

00:38:13.640 --> 00:38:16.800
by the models that are
in your leaderboards

00:38:16.800 --> 00:38:21.720
and by having pairwise
comparisons between them.

00:38:21.720 --> 00:38:26.280
And we saw that there was a
particular way of measuring

00:38:26.280 --> 00:38:30.600
performance that took
into consideration

00:38:30.600 --> 00:38:33.700
the history of each model.

00:38:33.700 --> 00:38:40.640
And by history, I mean how
people rated that model.

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:43.240
And the reason
for that is we saw

00:38:43.240 --> 00:38:48.190
that if you were to win
against a weak week model,

00:38:48.190 --> 00:38:49.870
it is not the same
thing as if you were

00:38:49.870 --> 00:38:54.670
to win against a strong model.

00:38:54.670 --> 00:38:57.070
So you want to take
into consideration

00:38:57.070 --> 00:39:01.230
how strong your opponent
is, otherwise, a metric

00:39:01.230 --> 00:39:05.630
like the win rates would be
very dependent on who you are

00:39:05.630 --> 00:39:08.630
making your comparisons with.

00:39:08.630 --> 00:39:12.150
And we saw this
metric called the Elo

00:39:12.150 --> 00:39:17.790
rating, Elo score that looked
at this in the following way.

00:39:17.790 --> 00:39:23.610
So if you were to rate different
models, so you have a ratings,

00:39:23.610 --> 00:39:30.190
let's call it R, that quantifies
how good each model is.

00:39:30.190 --> 00:39:35.110
So in order for you to--

00:39:35.110 --> 00:39:36.650
let's suppose you
have a new model.

00:39:36.650 --> 00:39:38.830
You want to compute its rating.

00:39:38.830 --> 00:39:42.830
So what you would do is
compare that new model to,

00:39:42.830 --> 00:39:46.670
let's say, a model that
is part of the list.

00:39:46.670 --> 00:39:52.070
And you would use the
rating of the models

00:39:52.070 --> 00:39:56.070
to quantify how good
they are, and take that

00:39:56.070 --> 00:39:58.830
into consideration
in order to find

00:39:58.830 --> 00:40:01.590
the rating of the new model.

00:40:01.590 --> 00:40:02.950
So how do you do that?

00:40:02.950 --> 00:40:05.670
Well, the idea is quite simple.

00:40:05.670 --> 00:40:11.150
So the ratings quantify
how good models are.

00:40:11.150 --> 00:40:14.790
So from that quantity,
what you can do

00:40:14.790 --> 00:40:20.210
is to compute what you
expect will happen.

00:40:20.210 --> 00:40:24.070
So for instance, if you have a
strong model and a weak model,

00:40:24.070 --> 00:40:28.190
well, what you expect is
the strong model to win.

00:40:28.190 --> 00:40:29.670
So you compute a
quantity which is

00:40:29.670 --> 00:40:35.070
the expected score, which is a
function of these two ratings.

00:40:35.070 --> 00:40:37.610
And you compare that
with the actual score.

00:40:40.470 --> 00:40:42.830
And the difference between
that, which is here,

00:40:42.830 --> 00:40:47.460
delta, tells you how
surprised you are.

00:40:47.460 --> 00:40:50.820
So if you won against
a strong model, then

00:40:50.820 --> 00:40:53.260
you want your delta to be high.

00:40:53.260 --> 00:40:56.020
And if you won against
a weak model, then

00:40:56.020 --> 00:41:00.540
it's not a lot of signal for
you because almost everyone

00:41:00.540 --> 00:41:02.320
wins against that weak model.

00:41:02.320 --> 00:41:05.100
So delta would be lower.

00:41:05.100 --> 00:41:09.100
So all of that to say that
you will come across the Elo

00:41:09.100 --> 00:41:10.900
score quite a lot.

00:41:10.900 --> 00:41:15.940
And I just want you to
remember that the Elo score is

00:41:15.940 --> 00:41:21.620
a smart way of computing
pairwise comparisons

00:41:21.620 --> 00:41:26.520
by taking into consideration
how strong your opponent is,

00:41:26.520 --> 00:41:31.460
if you compare model
A and model B. Cool.

00:41:31.460 --> 00:41:34.900
But not everyone has the
luxury of having human ratings

00:41:34.900 --> 00:41:36.260
for everything they do.

00:41:36.260 --> 00:41:41.890
So that's why we also have a
bunch of automated metrics.

00:41:41.890 --> 00:41:46.670
So we looked at one that
was quite popular nowadays,

00:41:46.670 --> 00:41:49.330
which is called the FID score--

00:41:49.330 --> 00:41:51.930
Fréchet inception distance.

00:41:51.930 --> 00:41:57.250
So what this score
does is it computes

00:41:57.250 --> 00:42:01.210
the distance between
the distribution

00:42:01.210 --> 00:42:07.410
of your generated images and
the distribution of real images.

00:42:07.410 --> 00:42:12.130
And the idea is if these
distributions are far,

00:42:12.130 --> 00:42:15.210
then it means that
your generated images,

00:42:15.210 --> 00:42:19.490
they don't look
like real images.

00:42:19.490 --> 00:42:23.210
And we saw that the
FID score-- so I didn't

00:42:23.210 --> 00:42:25.810
put the formula here,
but it is something

00:42:25.810 --> 00:42:30.450
that is derived from a
more general formulation.

00:42:30.450 --> 00:42:34.890
And here, the actual
formula is derived

00:42:34.890 --> 00:42:38.570
considering that these
two distributions are

00:42:38.570 --> 00:42:41.400
considered as Gaussian.

00:42:41.400 --> 00:42:43.680
So that's the
assumption that we're

00:42:43.680 --> 00:42:47.040
doing when we're
computing this metric.

00:42:47.040 --> 00:42:51.960
And we saw that the lower this
metric was, the better it was.

00:42:51.960 --> 00:42:53.460
And of course, it's a proxy.

00:42:53.460 --> 00:42:57.760
So it's not a perfect metric.

00:42:57.760 --> 00:43:04.920
And then we looked at more
clever ways of obtaining scores,

00:43:04.920 --> 00:43:09.980
in particular, by leveraging
these newer models.

00:43:09.980 --> 00:43:14.320
So here, MLLM, which stands
for multi-modal large language

00:43:14.320 --> 00:43:21.000
models that are able to
both take texts, but also

00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:22.580
images as input.

00:43:22.580 --> 00:43:25.820
So you could very well
say, you have this image.

00:43:25.820 --> 00:43:27.260
You have this input prompt.

00:43:27.260 --> 00:43:31.440
Well, tell me how it looks
like or how good that is.

00:43:31.440 --> 00:43:34.520
Then you could obtain
some given scores.

00:43:34.520 --> 00:43:40.680
And we saw that you also had
a way to leverage these models

00:43:40.680 --> 00:43:46.000
in order for you to not have
to ask your humans to rate

00:43:46.000 --> 00:43:50.160
your images, but to ask
your MLLM as a judge to rate

00:43:50.160 --> 00:43:51.440
them for you.

00:43:51.440 --> 00:43:54.200
You could have a
tighter loop that

00:43:54.200 --> 00:43:58.360
would allow you to make
iterations faster and have

00:43:58.360 --> 00:44:02.400
human ratings when you feel
like your model is good enough

00:44:02.400 --> 00:44:06.720
for you to take that step.

00:44:06.720 --> 00:44:10.700
And this was CME 296.

00:44:10.700 --> 00:44:13.680
So you see that in
order for us to generate

00:44:13.680 --> 00:44:18.760
an image from a prompt, there
is a lot that goes underneath.

00:44:18.760 --> 00:44:25.680
And this is what the whole
lectures 1 to 7 were about.

00:44:28.360 --> 00:44:29.220
Cool.

00:44:29.220 --> 00:44:33.200
So now you finish this
class after this lecture

00:44:33.200 --> 00:44:35.200
and you're like, OK,
now I know all of this.

00:44:35.200 --> 00:44:36.750
And so what?

00:44:36.750 --> 00:44:41.350
So I wanted to talk to you about
the state of the art models that

00:44:41.350 --> 00:44:42.910
were out there.

00:44:42.910 --> 00:44:52.190
So what we did was just go
to a popular arena that ranks

00:44:52.190 --> 00:44:53.890
the best models out there.

00:44:53.890 --> 00:44:54.990
So there's a leaderboard.

00:44:54.990 --> 00:44:57.630
And as you can see,
there is the Elo score

00:44:57.630 --> 00:45:01.110
that is used to
determine those ranks.

00:45:01.110 --> 00:45:07.350
And I just wanted for us to look
at which models were the best

00:45:07.350 --> 00:45:11.950
and whether they were also
using the methods that we

00:45:11.950 --> 00:45:15.750
saw in this class or not.

00:45:15.750 --> 00:45:19.330
So let's start with the
best models out there,

00:45:19.330 --> 00:45:21.350
and we see that we
have the first two

00:45:21.350 --> 00:45:22.970
ranks that are from OpenAI.

00:45:22.970 --> 00:45:24.350
So GPT Image.

00:45:24.350 --> 00:45:27.350
And then we have two
models from Google.

00:45:27.350 --> 00:45:28.830
And then the fourth--

00:45:28.830 --> 00:45:31.730
sorry, the fifth
model is from xAI.

00:45:31.730 --> 00:45:37.860
So it's all closed-source
models from top AI labs.

00:45:37.860 --> 00:45:39.420
The problem with
those models is we

00:45:39.420 --> 00:45:41.900
don't how they work
because they're not

00:45:41.900 --> 00:45:44.140
publishing any reports.

00:45:44.140 --> 00:45:46.700
So there's not a
lot I can tell you.

00:45:46.700 --> 00:45:54.220
But that leaderboard also
ranks open weight models

00:45:54.220 --> 00:45:59.100
that have technical
reports that are published

00:45:59.100 --> 00:46:02.460
that are public and available.

00:46:02.460 --> 00:46:06.500
So it's a screenshot that
dates from five days ago.

00:46:06.500 --> 00:46:09.860
And we see that the
highest ranked model is

00:46:09.860 --> 00:46:13.600
from a company called HiDream.

00:46:13.600 --> 00:46:16.280
So it's HiDream-01-Image
number one.

00:46:16.280 --> 00:46:21.380
And then you have Qwen Image
that we saw in lecture 5,

00:46:21.380 --> 00:46:24.380
when we talked about the
multi-modal diffusion

00:46:24.380 --> 00:46:26.300
transformer.

00:46:26.300 --> 00:46:30.980
And then the ranks three
to five are from this model

00:46:30.980 --> 00:46:35.300
from Black Forest
Lab called FLUX 2.

00:46:35.300 --> 00:46:38.980
And what I wanted us to do
was to go through these models

00:46:38.980 --> 00:46:43.460
and see what they were made of.

00:46:43.460 --> 00:46:47.700
So let's start with the last
ones, the FLUX 2 models.

00:46:47.700 --> 00:46:53.580
So they're based on rectified
flow, which, as I mentioned,

00:46:53.580 --> 00:46:56.540
is a derivative
of flow matching.

00:46:56.540 --> 00:46:59.530
So something that we know.

00:46:59.530 --> 00:47:04.800
Their architecture is based
on the diffusion transformer.

00:47:04.800 --> 00:47:09.620
But in particular, what our
architecture is based on

00:47:09.620 --> 00:47:14.700
is a combination
of single stream

00:47:14.700 --> 00:47:17.580
and double-stream
diffusion transformer.

00:47:17.580 --> 00:47:21.260
So this is also
something that we saw.

00:47:21.260 --> 00:47:23.700
So they rely on a
VAE to make sure

00:47:23.700 --> 00:47:29.900
that the latent space is compact
and allows you to learn easily.

00:47:29.900 --> 00:47:31.930
So that's also
something that we saw.

00:47:31.930 --> 00:47:35.890
And we also happen
to have some text

00:47:35.890 --> 00:47:37.990
encoder that was pre-trained.

00:47:37.990 --> 00:47:38.490
And here.

00:47:38.490 --> 00:47:45.770
It's a Mistral 3, that is used
to generate the embeddings.

00:47:45.770 --> 00:47:48.530
So up until now, the usual.

00:47:51.690 --> 00:47:54.490
Let's go to the model number 2.

00:47:54.490 --> 00:47:56.490
So Qwen Image.

00:47:56.490 --> 00:47:58.470
The loss is a flow
matching loss.

00:47:58.470 --> 00:48:01.570
So also something that we know.

00:48:01.570 --> 00:48:05.690
So the architecture is
a multi-modal diffusion

00:48:05.690 --> 00:48:07.650
transformer.

00:48:07.650 --> 00:48:10.210
And it also relies on a VAE.

00:48:10.210 --> 00:48:15.570
And the text embeddings
are based on Qwen.

00:48:15.570 --> 00:48:17.370
Also the usual.

00:48:17.370 --> 00:48:23.590
Now let's look at the top-ranked
model, which by the way,

00:48:23.590 --> 00:48:25.890
was published, I
think, two weeks ago.

00:48:25.890 --> 00:48:28.520
So it's extremely recent.

00:48:28.520 --> 00:48:36.000
So flow matching loss also
similar to what we saw.

00:48:36.000 --> 00:48:39.460
The model is not exactly
a multi-modal diffusion

00:48:39.460 --> 00:48:40.960
transformer, but
it's something that

00:48:40.960 --> 00:48:42.340
is based on the transformer.

00:48:42.340 --> 00:48:45.800
So also very similar.

00:48:45.800 --> 00:48:51.060
But turns out that
there is no more VAE.

00:48:53.600 --> 00:48:58.380
And there is no
pre-trained text encoder.

00:49:01.160 --> 00:49:05.100
So does that mean that what
we learned was not true?

00:49:05.100 --> 00:49:07.280
What's going on?

00:49:07.280 --> 00:49:12.800
So let's see how that
relates to what we learned.

00:49:12.800 --> 00:49:18.320
So that paper, what it does
is it actually performs

00:49:18.320 --> 00:49:25.240
the generation, not in a latent
space, but in pixel space.

00:49:25.240 --> 00:49:30.170
And now you may think, well,
how is the dimensionality going?

00:49:30.170 --> 00:49:32.990
Because it's much higher
than a latent space.

00:49:32.990 --> 00:49:34.910
So that's the first question.

00:49:34.910 --> 00:49:37.990
And then the second
question is pixel space

00:49:37.990 --> 00:49:40.990
is much harder for you
to learn because we

00:49:40.990 --> 00:49:47.790
saw that in the space, valid
images in the pixel space

00:49:47.790 --> 00:49:50.210
are more isolated.

00:49:50.210 --> 00:49:53.950
They're not smooth distribution.

00:49:53.950 --> 00:49:57.790
Well, here is my interpretation.

00:49:57.790 --> 00:50:00.830
So first of all, for
the dimensionality.

00:50:00.830 --> 00:50:07.150
So it turns out that the patch
size that the paper uses is not

00:50:07.150 --> 00:50:11.290
the traditional 2 by 2 that
we're used to for latent space.

00:50:11.290 --> 00:50:13.190
It's actually a much bigger one.

00:50:13.190 --> 00:50:17.270
So it's a 32 by 32.

00:50:17.270 --> 00:50:20.070
So computationally
here, the idea

00:50:20.070 --> 00:50:25.430
is to make it tractable by
just having bigger patches.

00:50:25.430 --> 00:50:29.670
And then when it
comes to learnability,

00:50:29.670 --> 00:50:36.070
well, the fact of not having
an easy space to learn from

00:50:36.070 --> 00:50:40.110
is just shifting the
effort that needs

00:50:40.110 --> 00:50:45.230
to happen over to the
diffusion-based transformer.

00:50:45.230 --> 00:50:49.790
And so what I found very
interesting with this paper was

00:50:49.790 --> 00:50:54.110
even if you make your problem
harder for your diffusion

00:50:54.110 --> 00:50:58.330
transformer or your
transformer-based model, well,

00:50:58.330 --> 00:51:00.390
you can still have
amazing results,

00:51:00.390 --> 00:51:03.150
if you scale it well enough.

00:51:03.150 --> 00:51:04.990
Because that model,
well, turns out

00:51:04.990 --> 00:51:06.910
that it was scaled, I
believe, to 8 billion

00:51:06.910 --> 00:51:10.270
and then also to 200
billion parameters, which

00:51:10.270 --> 00:51:14.310
is huge for image
generation models.

00:51:14.310 --> 00:51:20.710
And so what I wanted to say was
that the space is fast moving,

00:51:20.710 --> 00:51:24.300
that what we talked
about still holds true,

00:51:24.300 --> 00:51:29.620
but that the trade off
between making it easier

00:51:29.620 --> 00:51:34.580
for the transformer to learn
versus doing it in the raw space

00:51:34.580 --> 00:51:37.735
is still something that was
being figured out because there

00:51:37.735 --> 00:51:39.360
is something that I
have not mentioned,

00:51:39.360 --> 00:51:43.620
which is when you
learn the latent space,

00:51:43.620 --> 00:51:48.340
so you're making it easier
for your model to learn.

00:51:48.340 --> 00:51:52.020
You're having a
more compact space.

00:51:52.020 --> 00:51:55.500
But then the main thing
that you're losing

00:51:55.500 --> 00:52:00.500
is fidelity, because it's
a lossy operation for you

00:52:00.500 --> 00:52:05.460
to operate in a space that
is not your original space.

00:52:05.460 --> 00:52:07.700
So when you know train
your VAE, you just

00:52:07.700 --> 00:52:11.440
hope that you will
reconstruct things truthfully.

00:52:11.440 --> 00:52:13.440
But it is not always the case.

00:52:13.440 --> 00:52:17.220
And it just turns
out that this paper

00:52:17.220 --> 00:52:24.610
shows that the
downside of the VAE

00:52:24.610 --> 00:52:28.290
are such that we could
maybe work without it,

00:52:28.290 --> 00:52:34.930
if you scale things
in a big enough way.

00:52:34.930 --> 00:52:36.150
So I found that interesting.

00:52:36.150 --> 00:52:40.230
And I think it's a newer trend
of doing things without VAEs.

00:52:40.230 --> 00:52:43.890
So I think it's just good for
us to keep an eye on this.

00:52:43.890 --> 00:52:47.250
So again, this paper was
published a few weeks ago.

00:52:47.250 --> 00:52:50.010
I don't want to make general
statements because maybe

00:52:50.010 --> 00:52:53.210
in a couple of months,
then we will see maybe

00:52:53.210 --> 00:52:54.910
that the VAE is necessary.

00:52:54.910 --> 00:53:00.690
But I think this is a part
that is worth watching.

00:53:00.690 --> 00:53:01.750
Cool.

00:53:01.750 --> 00:53:05.690
So any questions on that?

00:53:05.690 --> 00:53:07.250
Yeah.

00:53:07.250 --> 00:53:07.790
Oh, yeah.

00:53:07.790 --> 00:53:09.930
So the question is,
what is the alternative

00:53:09.930 --> 00:53:11.430
to pre-trained encoder?

00:53:11.430 --> 00:53:14.810
So the alternative is to
just learn it yourself.

00:53:14.810 --> 00:53:19.890
So instead of having
a pre-trained encoder

00:53:19.890 --> 00:53:22.770
computing the embeddings
for you, what you do

00:53:22.770 --> 00:53:26.250
is you take the text as input.

00:53:26.250 --> 00:53:28.610
What you do is you
just tokenize it.

00:53:28.610 --> 00:53:33.450
So you divide it into
arbitrary units of text.

00:53:33.450 --> 00:53:37.690
And you learn the representation
as part of your training.

00:53:37.690 --> 00:53:40.350
And so there's one
thing I didn't say here,

00:53:40.350 --> 00:53:45.410
but they're not using a
pre-trained text encoder

00:53:45.410 --> 00:53:51.850
to do that, but they're doing
some kind of prompt enhancement

00:53:51.850 --> 00:53:55.630
that allows you to be more
explicit about the input prompt,

00:53:55.630 --> 00:54:00.210
because you may have a prompt
like a teddy bear is reading.

00:54:00.210 --> 00:54:03.110
But in order to generate
a very good image,

00:54:03.110 --> 00:54:07.450
you need to know, for instance,
how the lighting should be,

00:54:07.450 --> 00:54:11.790
what camera position
should be, and so on.

00:54:11.790 --> 00:54:16.570
And so that piece is also making
the job of the text encoder

00:54:16.570 --> 00:54:21.120
easier because it
just explicits what

00:54:21.120 --> 00:54:22.660
you need to have as condition.

00:54:25.200 --> 00:54:26.840
Yeah.

00:54:26.840 --> 00:54:30.620
So the question is, if there
is no disjoint text encoder,

00:54:30.620 --> 00:54:32.420
how are they able
to understand text?

00:54:32.420 --> 00:54:37.000
So here the key
word is pre-trained.

00:54:37.000 --> 00:54:39.720
So there's still a text encoder,
but it's just not something

00:54:39.720 --> 00:54:42.320
that you take off the shelf.

00:54:42.320 --> 00:54:45.400
It is something that
you train yourself

00:54:45.400 --> 00:54:46.620
as part of the training.

00:54:49.600 --> 00:54:51.080
OK, cool.

00:54:51.080 --> 00:54:55.320
So this concludes part 1,
which was around recapping

00:54:55.320 --> 00:54:59.080
what we did up until now.

00:54:59.080 --> 00:55:05.360
So now that we know all of
this, what I want us to do

00:55:05.360 --> 00:55:10.080
is to see how adjacent
fields can benefit

00:55:10.080 --> 00:55:12.320
from what we have learned.

00:55:12.320 --> 00:55:16.550
And a very natural extension
that I want to talk to you about

00:55:16.550 --> 00:55:20.430
is videos.

00:55:20.430 --> 00:55:23.470
So if you think
about it, videos are

00:55:23.470 --> 00:55:28.750
nothing else than a sequence
of frames which are images.

00:55:28.750 --> 00:55:31.590
So you just have
an extra dimension.

00:55:31.590 --> 00:55:41.630
So here you go from a 2D image
to a 3D image across time.

00:55:41.630 --> 00:55:44.950
And the question now for
us is, what should we

00:55:44.950 --> 00:55:50.270
have in mind when we want to
build a video generation model?

00:55:50.270 --> 00:55:52.310
And what are the
things we can use

00:55:52.310 --> 00:55:54.790
from what we have
learned in order for us

00:55:54.790 --> 00:55:56.840
to be able to do that?

00:55:56.840 --> 00:55:58.590
So the first thing
that I want to tell you

00:55:58.590 --> 00:56:03.830
is there is an extra
dimension, which is time.

00:56:03.830 --> 00:56:07.950
The second thing that I want
to talk to you about is that

00:56:07.950 --> 00:56:14.180
in a video, you need to make
sure that there is some temporal

00:56:14.180 --> 00:56:19.980
consistency, as in you cannot
go from one frame to the other

00:56:19.980 --> 00:56:24.740
in a way that completely
changes what you're looking at.

00:56:24.740 --> 00:56:28.260
But you also need to
make sure that whoever

00:56:28.260 --> 00:56:33.260
is being depicted in your frames
do not have suddenly things

00:56:33.260 --> 00:56:35.122
that they did not have.

00:56:35.122 --> 00:56:36.580
So, for instance,
if you're looking

00:56:36.580 --> 00:56:39.200
at a video of a
teddy bear reading,

00:56:39.200 --> 00:56:43.980
you don't want that teddy bear
to all of a sudden acquire a hat

00:56:43.980 --> 00:56:50.220
and sunglasses at t plus 1, but
then at t not have any of those.

00:56:50.220 --> 00:56:55.680
So this is an extra thing
that you need to worry about.

00:56:55.680 --> 00:56:58.020
So for images, you
want it to generate

00:56:58.020 --> 00:57:01.260
something that was plausible.

00:57:01.260 --> 00:57:03.020
For videos, you need
to generate something

00:57:03.020 --> 00:57:04.980
that looks plausible
at a given time t,

00:57:04.980 --> 00:57:11.020
but you also need to make
sure that the way frames are

00:57:11.020 --> 00:57:13.360
sequenced is also making sense.

00:57:13.360 --> 00:57:15.480
So you need to have that
temporal consistency.

00:57:18.020 --> 00:57:23.220
And of course, you need to
make computations tractable

00:57:23.220 --> 00:57:25.300
because here, as
you can imagine,

00:57:25.300 --> 00:57:30.780
if we go the route of
representing our input

00:57:30.780 --> 00:57:35.620
as a sequence of
2D images, well,

00:57:35.620 --> 00:57:39.600
the dimension just got
increased by a factor of t,

00:57:39.600 --> 00:57:40.940
which is the time.

00:57:40.940 --> 00:57:44.780
And so you need to make sure
that whatever you're doing

00:57:44.780 --> 00:57:49.540
is making things
still tractable.

00:57:49.540 --> 00:57:51.820
And of course, metrics as well.

00:57:51.820 --> 00:57:56.040
And well, I'll just
get you the spoiler.

00:57:56.040 --> 00:57:59.920
So we still leverage the metrics
that we have seen for images,

00:57:59.920 --> 00:58:02.640
but we just extend
them for videos.

00:58:02.640 --> 00:58:05.100
So the Fréchet
inception distance--

00:58:05.100 --> 00:58:10.490
FID-- is something that you may
also see in the video worlds

00:58:10.490 --> 00:58:13.410
where the only
thing that changes

00:58:13.410 --> 00:58:17.690
is the representation
of the quantities

00:58:17.690 --> 00:58:19.490
that you're dealing with.

00:58:19.490 --> 00:58:23.090
So instead of having the
Inception network to represent

00:58:23.090 --> 00:58:26.130
your image, what
you're doing is you're

00:58:26.130 --> 00:58:32.170
using a pre-trained encoder that
would represent your videos.

00:58:32.170 --> 00:58:36.250
And you have, for instance, this
metric called the Fréchet video

00:58:36.250 --> 00:58:42.210
distance, that tells you how far
apart your generated videos are

00:58:42.210 --> 00:58:46.270
from real ones using
a similar method.

00:58:46.270 --> 00:58:48.610
But I want to call
out one thing.

00:58:48.610 --> 00:58:51.970
These metrics are just proxies.

00:58:51.970 --> 00:58:55.510
So it's always good to
have human in the loop.

00:58:58.270 --> 00:59:05.770
So now let's see how we can
handle just generating videos

00:59:05.770 --> 00:59:10.240
by going through specific
parts of the architecture

00:59:10.240 --> 00:59:13.160
that we may need to change.

00:59:13.160 --> 00:59:17.300
So if you think about it,
an image generation model,

00:59:17.300 --> 00:59:19.520
a traditional image
generation model

00:59:19.520 --> 00:59:26.920
is a DIT-based model
operating in a latent space.

00:59:26.920 --> 00:59:31.440
So you have a VAE that
compresses your input

00:59:31.440 --> 00:59:34.080
into some latent space.

00:59:34.080 --> 00:59:38.080
And then you obtain, let's
say, the final prediction

00:59:38.080 --> 00:59:42.720
that you then decode back
in your initial space.

00:59:42.720 --> 00:59:47.080
Well, the same thing is
happening for videos.

00:59:47.080 --> 00:59:51.080
So here, you not
only are compressing

00:59:51.080 --> 00:59:53.720
from a spatial
perspective, you're

00:59:53.720 --> 00:59:59.145
also performing a
temporal compression.

00:59:59.145 --> 01:00:00.520
So do you remember
when we talked

01:00:00.520 --> 01:00:05.960
about f, which was the
ratio of the heights

01:00:05.960 --> 01:00:11.240
in your initial space over the
height in your latent space?

01:00:11.240 --> 01:00:11.980
Do you remember?

01:00:11.980 --> 01:00:17.200
So maybe I'll just quickly--
so we had this pixel space.

01:00:17.200 --> 01:00:18.900
And then we have the
latent space here.

01:00:18.900 --> 01:00:25.240
And then you have
H and then h here.

01:00:25.240 --> 01:00:30.620
So we had f, the
spatial compression,

01:00:30.620 --> 01:00:35.040
which was just the ratio
of big H over little h.

01:00:35.040 --> 01:00:37.160
And that ratio is
typically on the order

01:00:37.160 --> 01:00:41.360
of 8 for spatial compression.

01:00:41.360 --> 01:00:46.320
Here what you also want to
do is to compress your input

01:00:46.320 --> 01:00:49.640
along the time
dimension as well.

01:00:49.640 --> 01:00:50.940
So, why do you want to do that?

01:00:50.940 --> 01:00:53.520
Well, the first
thing is you want

01:00:53.520 --> 01:00:58.720
to make sure your problem is
tractable from a dimensionality

01:00:58.720 --> 01:01:00.920
perspective.

01:01:00.920 --> 01:01:04.990
But then the second
reason is when

01:01:04.990 --> 01:01:10.990
you compress things in a smaller
space, what you're assuming

01:01:10.990 --> 01:01:15.030
is you have redundant
information.

01:01:15.030 --> 01:01:18.830
So spatially, we saw that
we have spatial correlation,

01:01:18.830 --> 01:01:21.550
meaning around some
pixels, you have

01:01:21.550 --> 01:01:23.650
pixels of the similar value.

01:01:23.650 --> 01:01:25.990
So you have redundant
information.

01:01:25.990 --> 01:01:30.710
Well, it's the same along
the time axis as well.

01:01:30.710 --> 01:01:32.870
Because you can
very well imagine

01:01:32.870 --> 01:01:37.310
that if you go through
the frames of a video,

01:01:37.310 --> 01:01:42.910
two consecutive frames will have
a lot of the same information.

01:01:42.910 --> 01:01:46.150
So it would also make sense
for you to compress things

01:01:46.150 --> 01:01:50.670
also along the time axis.

01:01:50.670 --> 01:01:53.090
And so here, this
compression ratio,

01:01:53.090 --> 01:01:55.990
which we saw was for
space, is also something

01:01:55.990 --> 01:01:59.590
that we have along
the time dimension.

01:01:59.590 --> 01:02:03.860
And as a result,
your latent space

01:02:03.860 --> 01:02:08.420
is composed, not
of space latent,

01:02:08.420 --> 01:02:12.620
but of space time latent.

01:02:12.620 --> 01:02:16.940
So what that means is
the quantities represent,

01:02:16.940 --> 01:02:21.480
not only things that are
within a given image,

01:02:21.480 --> 01:02:24.980
but also with respect to time.

01:02:24.980 --> 01:02:28.980
So there is one thing that
you may find interesting,

01:02:28.980 --> 01:02:35.020
which is that the dimension
over t is 1 plus t

01:02:35.020 --> 01:02:41.860
And then the latent space is
of dimension 1 over t over 4.

01:02:41.860 --> 01:02:44.980
But then you may
ask, why the 1 plus?

01:02:44.980 --> 01:02:46.698
Why 1?

01:02:46.698 --> 01:02:48.240
And I would say it's
a good question.

01:02:48.240 --> 01:02:50.020
It's also a question
I ask myself.

01:02:50.020 --> 01:02:55.260
Well, it turns out that if
you want to generate a video,

01:02:55.260 --> 01:02:58.360
you need to have
a starting point.

01:02:58.360 --> 01:03:01.050
You need to have
an initial frame.

01:03:01.050 --> 01:03:09.410
Well, that 1 is a way for us
to consider the first frame

01:03:09.410 --> 01:03:11.490
as being something special.

01:03:11.490 --> 01:03:13.410
It has a special place.

01:03:13.410 --> 01:03:15.230
It's the anchor frame.

01:03:15.230 --> 01:03:18.370
We want to make sure
we're representing

01:03:18.370 --> 01:03:22.130
it as well as possible.

01:03:22.130 --> 01:03:25.170
And so the reason why
we have the 1 plus

01:03:25.170 --> 01:03:29.170
is to represent that first
frame, that anchor frame

01:03:29.170 --> 01:03:34.050
to its fullest extent, and then
to use that as a way to continue

01:03:34.050 --> 01:03:38.410
the video in a way that
is a natural continuation

01:03:38.410 --> 01:03:39.470
of that first frame.

01:03:42.370 --> 01:03:43.350
Does that make sense?

01:03:46.850 --> 01:03:50.310
So by the way, we're going super
quickly over a lot of things.

01:03:50.310 --> 01:03:54.250
So my goal is to just
give you some pointers

01:03:54.250 --> 01:03:57.210
for if you're just interested,
let's say, after today,

01:03:57.210 --> 01:03:59.330
and you want to
just explore more,

01:03:59.330 --> 01:04:01.750
these are just some
small little things.

01:04:01.750 --> 01:04:04.450
The second thing I wanted
to talk about with respect

01:04:04.450 --> 01:04:10.390
to the VAE is so as we
said, it is not a 2D VAE.

01:04:10.390 --> 01:04:14.890
It's a 3D VAE, because it's
operating also along the time

01:04:14.890 --> 01:04:17.410
dimension.

01:04:17.410 --> 01:04:24.690
So you will see that VAE
is called a causal VAE.

01:04:24.690 --> 01:04:25.850
So why causal?

01:04:25.850 --> 01:04:30.050
So causal is because when
you perform, let's say,

01:04:30.050 --> 01:04:33.250
convolutions, which is typically
what one of the operations

01:04:33.250 --> 01:04:38.970
that VAE contains, you
perform your convolution

01:04:38.970 --> 01:04:42.250
in a symmetric manner.

01:04:42.250 --> 01:04:47.490
But here, what you want
to do for a given frame

01:04:47.490 --> 01:04:52.650
is to not compute
feature maps that are

01:04:52.650 --> 01:04:55.130
a function of future frames.

01:04:55.130 --> 01:04:58.120
You only want for
that frame to be

01:04:58.120 --> 01:05:02.960
a function of the same
frame and the frames before.

01:05:02.960 --> 01:05:05.260
So that is why it's
called a causal VAE.

01:05:05.260 --> 01:05:09.900
It's because the convolution
is not like the symmetric one

01:05:09.900 --> 01:05:10.900
that you're thinking of.

01:05:10.900 --> 01:05:13.640
It's asymmetric.

01:05:13.640 --> 01:05:17.120
And the reason why
people would do that

01:05:17.120 --> 01:05:18.540
is actually for several reasons.

01:05:18.540 --> 01:05:21.360
But one of the
reasons is you don't

01:05:21.360 --> 01:05:25.640
want to go out of memory every
time you do these things.

01:05:25.640 --> 01:05:27.360
So what people do
is sometimes they

01:05:27.360 --> 01:05:30.880
stream this encoding process.

01:05:30.880 --> 01:05:33.640
And if you make sure
that a given frame is not

01:05:33.640 --> 01:05:37.360
dependent on the future frames,
which by the way, the receptive

01:05:37.360 --> 01:05:43.040
field can widen if you have
several convolution operations.

01:05:43.040 --> 01:05:44.960
So if you make sure
that it only depends

01:05:44.960 --> 01:05:48.540
on that frame and the
frames before, then,

01:05:48.540 --> 01:05:51.280
computationally speaking,
you can actually

01:05:51.280 --> 01:05:56.116
stream that encoding
and decoding process.

01:05:56.116 --> 01:05:59.530
I'm just giving you reason why.

01:05:59.530 --> 01:06:01.190
And then the second
thing that I wanted

01:06:01.190 --> 01:06:04.370
to talk about with respect
to the video-- oh, yeah.

01:06:04.370 --> 01:06:05.330
You have a question?

01:06:08.865 --> 01:06:09.490
Great question.

01:06:09.490 --> 01:06:13.270
So the question is, why
would you need frames

01:06:13.270 --> 01:06:15.630
before the last frame?

01:06:15.630 --> 01:06:17.630
So let me give you an example.

01:06:17.630 --> 01:06:24.790
So let's suppose a teddy
bear walks across the street.

01:06:24.790 --> 01:06:27.130
And the camera looks
at that Teddy bear,

01:06:27.130 --> 01:06:31.790
and it sees a pedestrian
that is coming this way.

01:06:31.790 --> 01:06:37.030
And then later on, that
pedestrian comes back.

01:06:37.030 --> 01:06:42.790
So the information of who is
going behind the teddy bear

01:06:42.790 --> 01:06:45.030
is something that
is important to you.

01:06:45.030 --> 01:06:47.990
You want to be able
to capture things that

01:06:47.990 --> 01:06:50.350
makes your video consistent.

01:06:50.350 --> 01:06:53.790
So that temporal consistency,
which is making sure you're

01:06:53.790 --> 01:06:58.750
not inventing objects or
people in your video that

01:06:58.750 --> 01:07:01.750
may have happened in the past.

01:07:01.750 --> 01:07:05.950
So that's the reason why you
want your receptive fields

01:07:05.950 --> 01:07:08.310
to not be one.

01:07:08.310 --> 01:07:13.390
So you want to have some
dependency on earlier frames

01:07:13.390 --> 01:07:17.390
compared to the last one.

01:07:17.390 --> 01:07:21.110
Does that answer your question?

01:07:21.110 --> 01:07:23.530
The question is yeah
efficiency concerns.

01:07:23.530 --> 01:07:24.990
And yeah, I hear you.

01:07:24.990 --> 01:07:27.590
So there is something
we'll discuss

01:07:27.590 --> 01:07:30.710
which is exactly what is
happening in the latent space.

01:07:30.710 --> 01:07:34.690
So, of course, you cannot
generate a video of very,

01:07:34.690 --> 01:07:36.590
very big time t.

01:07:36.590 --> 01:07:40.830
So what people do is
divide the generation

01:07:40.830 --> 01:07:43.430
into several parts,
where you can

01:07:43.430 --> 01:07:46.550
generate a video of,
let's say, a fixed length,

01:07:46.550 --> 01:07:50.750
and then take the last
frame as the anchor frame

01:07:50.750 --> 01:07:54.540
to generate another video of,
let's say, another fixed length.

01:07:54.540 --> 01:07:58.060
So one way for you to
make this more tractable

01:07:58.060 --> 01:08:01.820
is to just take the
last frame of the video

01:08:01.820 --> 01:08:03.520
that you generated
as the first frame,

01:08:03.520 --> 01:08:07.780
and then continue later on
along with some condition.

01:08:07.780 --> 01:08:09.100
So yeah.

01:08:09.100 --> 01:08:11.060
Yeah.

01:08:11.060 --> 01:08:14.820
So the question
here is, how can you

01:08:14.820 --> 01:08:20.740
teach your model how to
generate the next frame, given

01:08:20.740 --> 01:08:21.819
some conditions?

01:08:21.819 --> 01:08:24.939
So we're actually going to
see that in the next slide.

01:08:24.939 --> 01:08:28.580
So this part is just about
compressing the inputs,

01:08:28.580 --> 01:08:31.779
but we're not yet
generating the video.

01:08:31.779 --> 01:08:34.260
So we're going to see
that in just one second.

01:08:34.260 --> 01:08:35.479
But yeah, great question.

01:08:39.060 --> 01:08:39.600
Cool.

01:08:39.600 --> 01:08:41.260
Any other questions?

01:08:41.260 --> 01:08:45.220
So I'll answer your
question right now.

01:08:45.220 --> 01:08:51.609
The way we generate videos
is within that latent space,

01:08:51.609 --> 01:08:55.130
similar to how we do it
for image generation,

01:08:55.130 --> 01:08:58.890
we have this
DiT-based architecture

01:08:58.890 --> 01:09:04.850
that works from patches
and that generates patches.

01:09:04.850 --> 01:09:09.490
The main difference from
images is that these patches,

01:09:09.490 --> 01:09:12.189
they are not space patches.

01:09:12.189 --> 01:09:13.470
They're not spatial patches.

01:09:13.470 --> 01:09:16.370
They're space time patches.

01:09:16.370 --> 01:09:21.649
And what that means
is as input, we have,

01:09:21.649 --> 01:09:29.665
if you want something that
represents some part of the,

01:09:29.665 --> 01:09:31.290
let's say, image,
it's not really image

01:09:31.290 --> 01:09:35.810
because you're in the
latent space across time.

01:09:35.810 --> 01:09:41.609
And in order for you
to think that you're

01:09:41.609 --> 01:09:44.450
doing a good job at
producing a video that

01:09:44.450 --> 01:09:47.170
is consistent with
oneself, one thing

01:09:47.170 --> 01:09:49.920
that you can tell
yourself when you're

01:09:49.920 --> 01:09:55.280
looking at that whole process
is that within the DiT,

01:09:55.280 --> 01:09:59.280
you have the self-attention
mechanism that allows your space

01:09:59.280 --> 01:10:02.080
time patches to interact
with one another

01:10:02.080 --> 01:10:04.640
so that you're
producing an output that

01:10:04.640 --> 01:10:10.000
is coherent across space,
but also across time.

01:10:10.000 --> 01:10:14.760
So I think your question
also involved some amount of,

01:10:14.760 --> 01:10:19.700
how can you make sure that
it reflects causality?

01:10:19.700 --> 01:10:24.360
So can you expand on what
you mean by causality?

01:10:24.360 --> 01:10:26.760
So the question is,
how can you make

01:10:26.760 --> 01:10:29.800
sure you have some causality
versus correlation?

01:10:29.800 --> 01:10:31.800
I think at the end of
the day, what you're

01:10:31.800 --> 01:10:34.760
doing is you're training
your model on some data,

01:10:34.760 --> 01:10:39.200
and the resulting model is just
reflecting the patterns that it

01:10:39.200 --> 01:10:41.280
has seen at training time.

01:10:41.280 --> 01:10:45.640
So if you feed it things that
reflect, for instance, if you

01:10:45.640 --> 01:10:50.185
put a book on a table,
and there's some dust that

01:10:50.185 --> 01:10:51.560
goes away, these
are the patterns

01:10:51.560 --> 01:10:53.640
that your model will also learn.

01:10:53.640 --> 01:10:55.160
So I think at the
end of the day,

01:10:55.160 --> 01:11:00.560
it all goes back to what
kind of data you put inside.

01:11:00.560 --> 01:11:03.760
So that's how I
would think about it.

01:11:03.760 --> 01:11:05.196
Yeah.

01:11:05.196 --> 01:11:07.680
Yeah.

01:11:07.680 --> 01:11:10.360
So the question is,
well, we know in LLMs,

01:11:10.360 --> 01:11:14.100
we have a masked self-attention
that makes things causal.

01:11:14.100 --> 01:11:17.200
Maybe there is something
that we can reuse here.

01:11:17.200 --> 01:11:20.160
Well, in the image
generation world,

01:11:20.160 --> 01:11:23.440
we're making sure that
all parts of the image,

01:11:23.440 --> 01:11:25.760
they interact with one another.

01:11:25.760 --> 01:11:29.480
And this is actually something
that we also have here,

01:11:29.480 --> 01:11:33.200
the main reason
being that you want

01:11:33.200 --> 01:11:38.200
some consistency between
every part of your video.

01:11:38.200 --> 01:11:41.040
And so for that reason,
people typically

01:11:41.040 --> 01:11:44.790
keep the full self-attention.

01:11:44.790 --> 01:11:46.350
But then at the
end of the day, you

01:11:46.350 --> 01:11:48.370
need to try it out
and see how it works.

01:11:48.370 --> 01:11:52.490
[LAUGHS] But typically people
keep the self-attention.

01:11:52.490 --> 01:11:52.990
Yeah.

01:11:56.670 --> 01:11:57.530
OK, great.

01:11:57.530 --> 01:12:01.970
So that model that we just saw
is a model that is open weights.

01:12:01.970 --> 01:12:03.710
It's called Wan.

01:12:03.710 --> 01:12:07.110
There are actually a
multiple models out there.

01:12:07.110 --> 01:12:11.790
And this list of readings is
by no means comprehensive.

01:12:11.790 --> 01:12:13.990
So in case you're
interested, feel

01:12:13.990 --> 01:12:17.190
free to just take
a look at those.

01:12:17.190 --> 01:12:19.650
So I know Wan is one such model.

01:12:19.650 --> 01:12:24.070
I believe there's
also LTX as well.

01:12:24.070 --> 01:12:26.570
Highly recommend
reading the papers.

01:12:26.570 --> 01:12:30.270
I would say that given
the fact that you

01:12:30.270 --> 01:12:35.150
know how image generation models
work, it's now much, much easier

01:12:35.150 --> 01:12:36.990
to understand these papers.

01:12:36.990 --> 01:12:41.870
So hopefully that's
going to be helpful.

01:12:41.870 --> 01:12:42.560
Cool.

01:12:42.560 --> 01:12:47.900
So video generation was
once adjacent fields

01:12:47.900 --> 01:12:50.300
that I wanted us to touch on.

01:12:50.300 --> 01:12:53.420
The second topic that I
wanted us to talk about

01:12:53.420 --> 01:12:57.060
is image editing.

01:12:57.060 --> 01:13:02.140
So let's imagine that
you have an image,

01:13:02.140 --> 01:13:06.240
and what you want to do
is to edit that image.

01:13:06.240 --> 01:13:10.940
So you can say, make this
image black and white.

01:13:10.940 --> 01:13:13.700
So you could very
well use what we

01:13:13.700 --> 01:13:18.660
have learned, which is you
put that into your text image

01:13:18.660 --> 01:13:19.560
to image model.

01:13:19.560 --> 01:13:20.980
So ti to i.

01:13:20.980 --> 01:13:22.040
So, how do you do that?

01:13:22.040 --> 01:13:23.720
Well, you represent
your condition,

01:13:23.720 --> 01:13:28.580
which is the text and the
image as part of, let's say,

01:13:28.580 --> 01:13:30.660
your MMDiT, and you inject that.

01:13:30.660 --> 01:13:34.580
And you can obtain
an output image.

01:13:34.580 --> 01:13:38.580
Well, the problem
here is that you're

01:13:38.580 --> 01:13:44.460
considering this problem a from
scratch generation problem.

01:13:44.460 --> 01:13:48.540
So you're telling your model,
make this image black and white.

01:13:48.540 --> 01:13:51.460
And what you're expecting
is that exact same image,

01:13:51.460 --> 01:13:53.580
but black and white.

01:13:53.580 --> 01:14:00.060
And you can just ask yourself,
is it actually optimized

01:14:00.060 --> 01:14:00.920
to what I'm doing?

01:14:00.920 --> 01:14:02.740
So what I'm doing is
I'm asking a model

01:14:02.740 --> 01:14:05.940
to generate a whole
thing from scratch.

01:14:05.940 --> 01:14:10.100
And I have no guarantee
that it's the same image.

01:14:10.100 --> 01:14:15.500
So the question here, is
there a better way for image

01:14:15.500 --> 01:14:19.660
editing tasks to
edit your image?

01:14:19.660 --> 01:14:22.940
Specifically, in cases where
you want the input image to be

01:14:22.940 --> 01:14:27.060
preserved, is there a better
way to edit those images

01:14:27.060 --> 01:14:32.020
than to consider this from
scratch generation problem?

01:14:32.020 --> 01:14:36.700
Well, so in this case, you see
that the black and white image

01:14:36.700 --> 01:14:41.130
had the teddy bear
raise its right arm.

01:14:41.130 --> 01:14:43.410
And this is not what you want.

01:14:43.410 --> 01:14:47.730
So people have been
thinking about this problem

01:14:47.730 --> 01:14:50.930
from another angle.

01:14:50.930 --> 01:14:54.650
So what they're saying is
instead of considering this

01:14:54.650 --> 01:15:00.730
as an image generation
problem, let's actually

01:15:00.730 --> 01:15:05.190
think about this problem as
an image editing problem.

01:15:05.190 --> 01:15:09.730
And by editing, I mean,
in the sense of performing

01:15:09.730 --> 01:15:14.290
some operations on the image.

01:15:14.290 --> 01:15:20.290
So one idea could be
to have your image,

01:15:20.290 --> 01:15:25.770
to have your prompt
be fed into a VLM.

01:15:25.770 --> 01:15:29.410
So vision language
model, which is an MLLM--

01:15:29.410 --> 01:15:33.210
multimodal large language model.

01:15:33.210 --> 01:15:39.680
In order for you to receive
live editing actions that

01:15:39.680 --> 01:15:44.120
could be maybe decrease the
brightness by, I don't know,

01:15:44.120 --> 01:15:49.720
50%, just increase the
luminosity by x percent

01:15:49.720 --> 01:15:55.320
that you could then interact
with an editing software,

01:15:55.320 --> 01:16:00.020
like Photoshop, in order
to get your final image.

01:16:03.640 --> 01:16:08.820
So the nice thing with this
approach is, well, first of all,

01:16:08.820 --> 01:16:11.320
you have some guarantee
that you are preserving

01:16:11.320 --> 01:16:13.680
your initial image,
if, let's say,

01:16:13.680 --> 01:16:18.400
you're constraining the sets of
actions that you're taking to,

01:16:18.400 --> 01:16:21.160
let's say, an allow
list of actions

01:16:21.160 --> 01:16:26.920
that here can only be harmless,
like coloring actions.

01:16:26.920 --> 01:16:31.820
But one challenge
is for your VLM

01:16:31.820 --> 01:16:36.830
to know the set of
possible actions

01:16:36.830 --> 01:16:42.470
well enough for the editing
action to actually make sense.

01:16:42.470 --> 01:16:44.950
So that's the main challenge.

01:16:44.950 --> 01:16:47.590
And so that's why some people--

01:16:47.590 --> 01:16:49.670
I mean, when I say some
people, a lot of people

01:16:49.670 --> 01:16:52.210
are trying to think
about how to do this.

01:16:52.210 --> 01:16:58.170
So there's a very short list of
papers that try to resolve that.

01:16:58.170 --> 01:17:02.830
But there is one method
that I want to mention here,

01:17:02.830 --> 01:17:08.430
which is how do we want our
outputs-- like, how can we

01:17:08.430 --> 01:17:10.310
make sure that our
output is something

01:17:10.310 --> 01:17:13.430
that is aligned with
something that makes sense?

01:17:13.430 --> 01:17:21.270
Well, a possible method is
to look at the logs of people

01:17:21.270 --> 01:17:23.030
who are actually making edits.

01:17:23.030 --> 01:17:25.930
So let's assume you go to your
favorite editing software.

01:17:25.930 --> 01:17:27.290
You have an input image.

01:17:27.290 --> 01:17:32.750
And then you make some edits,
and then you have a final image.

01:17:32.750 --> 01:17:36.230
Well, the sequence of
edits that you made

01:17:36.230 --> 01:17:39.750
is something that your
model can learn from.

01:17:39.750 --> 01:17:43.760
Well, the only thing that
your model doesn't really know

01:17:43.760 --> 01:17:47.630
is what your intent was,
because you typically

01:17:47.630 --> 01:17:49.610
don't tell your
editing software, well,

01:17:49.610 --> 01:17:53.610
I want to make my
image more like this.

01:17:53.610 --> 01:17:56.550
You don't say that.

01:17:56.550 --> 01:17:59.590
So these papers, so most of
them, what they're trying to do

01:17:59.590 --> 01:18:09.950
is to come up with pairs of
initial image edited image,

01:18:09.950 --> 01:18:16.170
along with the user intent that
is inferred by these two images,

01:18:16.170 --> 01:18:18.590
let's say, from annotators.

01:18:18.590 --> 01:18:20.390
And what they're
doing is they're

01:18:20.390 --> 01:18:26.910
tuning the VLM on
these golden sets

01:18:26.910 --> 01:18:31.780
in order to have it
behave more like something

01:18:31.780 --> 01:18:38.380
that would tell you actions that
would correspond to your intent.

01:18:38.380 --> 01:18:40.480
So that's why you
have papers on this.

01:18:40.480 --> 01:18:43.460
It's just because that
in order for your model

01:18:43.460 --> 01:18:45.420
to learn how to do
these things, you

01:18:45.420 --> 01:18:48.680
need to do some work in
terms of which data to use,

01:18:48.680 --> 01:18:51.140
how you want to
train these things.

01:18:51.140 --> 01:18:53.760
And that's why they're here.

01:18:53.760 --> 01:18:55.320
So I highly recommend
reading them.

01:18:55.320 --> 01:18:57.900
It's an open area of research.

01:18:57.900 --> 01:19:01.280
If you take a look at the
years they were published,

01:19:01.280 --> 01:19:04.800
I mean, they were
published 2024, 2025, 2026.

01:19:04.800 --> 01:19:08.780
So extremely hot.

01:19:08.780 --> 01:19:09.600
Any questions?

01:19:09.600 --> 01:19:11.420
Yeah.

01:19:11.420 --> 01:19:16.100
So the question is, how can the
loss reflect the user intent?

01:19:16.100 --> 01:19:20.820
Well, the thing is you
are not using the loss

01:19:20.820 --> 01:19:23.140
to infer the user intent.

01:19:23.140 --> 01:19:27.500
So one possible way of
inferring user intent

01:19:27.500 --> 01:19:35.450
is to feed an off-the-shelf VLM
an initial image and an output

01:19:35.450 --> 01:19:40.850
image and to tell your
VLM, well, tell me

01:19:40.850 --> 01:19:43.210
what changed between
these two images

01:19:43.210 --> 01:19:47.270
in order to infer this box,
make this image black and white.

01:19:47.270 --> 01:19:49.210
So I'll give you an example.

01:19:49.210 --> 01:19:53.730
If we feed a VLM the colored
image and the black and white

01:19:53.730 --> 01:19:57.210
image, and we ask the
VLM, well, come up

01:19:57.210 --> 01:19:59.730
with something
that tells me what

01:19:59.730 --> 01:20:02.450
changed between
this and that image,

01:20:02.450 --> 01:20:06.050
it gives you the user intent.

01:20:06.050 --> 01:20:11.730
And then what you can do is
use that user intent along

01:20:11.730 --> 01:20:14.650
with the initial image.

01:20:14.650 --> 01:20:17.130
And then you can, of course,
collect the editing action

01:20:17.130 --> 01:20:22.250
that the user took and
tune your VLM to output

01:20:22.250 --> 01:20:26.170
these editing actions
given this input.

01:20:26.170 --> 01:20:29.510
So that's one popular way
I've been seeing these papers.

01:20:29.510 --> 01:20:30.410
[INAUDIBLE]

01:20:30.410 --> 01:20:33.250
Yeah.

01:20:33.250 --> 01:20:35.430
Cool.

01:20:35.430 --> 01:20:35.930
Great.

01:20:35.930 --> 01:20:38.250
So now we're in the
last part of what

01:20:38.250 --> 01:20:40.250
I wanted to talk to you about.

01:20:40.250 --> 01:20:43.010
And that part is
actually an area

01:20:43.010 --> 01:20:49.290
that I think is a very, very hot
area, which is how can we apply

01:20:49.290 --> 01:20:53.450
diffusion to the field of LLMs?

01:20:53.450 --> 01:20:59.130
So as you know, there has been
a lot of transfer of knowledge

01:20:59.130 --> 01:21:04.890
from what has worked well in the
text world to the vision world.

01:21:04.890 --> 01:21:08.430
So in the text world,
as you know, in 2017,

01:21:08.430 --> 01:21:10.890
we had the transformer
that was initially

01:21:10.890 --> 01:21:13.790
designed for translation tasks.

01:21:13.790 --> 01:21:15.570
So let's suppose you
want to translate

01:21:15.570 --> 01:21:17.890
from English to French.

01:21:17.890 --> 01:21:21.570
What you do is you use this
transformer to have some,

01:21:21.570 --> 01:21:24.890
let's say, English
text as inputs in order

01:21:24.890 --> 01:21:30.360
to have a generated
French text as output.

01:21:30.360 --> 01:21:33.580
And as you know, well,
people in the vision world,

01:21:33.580 --> 01:21:37.520
they adapted the
architecture in a way that

01:21:37.520 --> 01:21:40.560
leverages some of the
scalability benefits

01:21:40.560 --> 01:21:44.160
that the transformer
brings to the vision world.

01:21:44.160 --> 01:21:46.520
And in particular, there
is one architecture

01:21:46.520 --> 01:21:48.412
we have spent a
bunch of time on,

01:21:48.412 --> 01:21:50.120
which is the diffusion
transformer, which

01:21:50.120 --> 01:21:52.360
relies on that.

01:21:52.360 --> 01:21:54.120
So that's one thing.

01:21:54.120 --> 01:21:56.320
The next thing is
something we've briefly

01:21:56.320 --> 01:21:59.160
talked about during,
I believe, lecture

01:21:59.160 --> 01:22:04.000
6, which is around
post-training approaches,

01:22:04.000 --> 01:22:09.120
and in particular, the fact
of injecting negative signals

01:22:09.120 --> 01:22:10.640
into your model.

01:22:10.640 --> 01:22:15.360
So there is this
tuning method called

01:22:15.360 --> 01:22:18.240
DPO, which stands
for direct preference

01:22:18.240 --> 01:22:21.760
optimization from the LLM world,
that was something that was

01:22:21.760 --> 01:22:23.900
adapted to the diffusion world.

01:22:23.900 --> 01:22:25.330
So that's one.

01:22:25.330 --> 01:22:27.190
And another one that
you may be aware of

01:22:27.190 --> 01:22:32.230
is GRPO, which now is widely
in use in LLMs that is also

01:22:32.230 --> 01:22:36.310
something that
has been something

01:22:36.310 --> 01:22:38.510
that people have experimented
with in the vision

01:22:38.510 --> 01:22:41.510
world with flow GRPO.

01:22:41.510 --> 01:22:46.990
So now you can ask yourself, OK,
text has given so much to us.

01:22:46.990 --> 01:22:51.150
What can we give back to text?

01:22:51.150 --> 01:22:55.590
Well, I know this class is
not about text, so I will not

01:22:55.590 --> 01:22:56.850
go into the details.

01:22:56.850 --> 01:23:02.990
But one thing I want to talk
about is that in the text world,

01:23:02.990 --> 01:23:07.990
we are doing things mostly in an
autoregressive way, a little bit

01:23:07.990 --> 01:23:11.390
like when you and I,
when we talk, what we do

01:23:11.390 --> 01:23:13.870
is we generate one
word at a time.

01:23:13.870 --> 01:23:16.070
And whatever comes
out is a function

01:23:16.070 --> 01:23:18.790
of whatever came before.

01:23:18.790 --> 01:23:23.980
Just to illustrate this, so you
have your LLM, which here we say

01:23:23.980 --> 01:23:25.400
is autoregressive.

01:23:25.400 --> 01:23:29.100
Let's call it an ARM LLM,
autoregressive model.

01:23:29.100 --> 01:23:31.520
So here we have, let's
say, a token that says,

01:23:31.520 --> 01:23:33.440
OK, we're starting the sentence.

01:23:33.440 --> 01:23:38.420
So we say, A. And then
A goes into the model

01:23:38.420 --> 01:23:42.500
along with everything that
came before and a teddy.

01:23:42.500 --> 01:23:44.160
And we repeat it
again and again.

01:23:44.160 --> 01:23:48.420
Teddy bear is reading.

01:23:48.420 --> 01:23:53.460
We're generating sentences
by sequentially inputting

01:23:53.460 --> 01:23:57.060
tokens one at a time and having
the model look at anything

01:23:57.060 --> 01:23:59.660
that it has generated
before in order

01:23:59.660 --> 01:24:02.700
to generate the next token.

01:24:02.700 --> 01:24:09.700
Well, the problem with that is
that if your output sentence

01:24:09.700 --> 01:24:15.180
or your output
response is long, then

01:24:15.180 --> 01:24:19.980
you will take a lot of time
outputting that response

01:24:19.980 --> 01:24:23.700
because the number
of iterations here

01:24:23.700 --> 01:24:29.900
is in awe of the number
of tokens that you output.

01:24:29.900 --> 01:24:33.673
So in particular, I'm not
sure if you do a lot of coding

01:24:33.673 --> 01:24:36.340
on a day-to-day basis, but let's
suppose you want to, let's say,

01:24:36.340 --> 01:24:40.300
code a huge thing, like
1,000 lines of code.

01:24:40.300 --> 01:24:43.620
Well, what that model
is doing is really

01:24:43.620 --> 01:24:46.860
outputting things one at a time.

01:24:46.860 --> 01:24:48.380
It takes a long time.

01:24:48.380 --> 01:24:52.580
And of course, you
want this to be quick.

01:24:52.580 --> 01:25:03.380
Well, one idea is to borrow
this idea of diffusion for text.

01:25:03.380 --> 01:25:07.020
So here what you can
think of is instead

01:25:07.020 --> 01:25:13.060
of doing things one at a time,
how about we do everything

01:25:13.060 --> 01:25:14.880
at once.

01:25:14.880 --> 01:25:16.940
And we start from noise.

01:25:16.940 --> 01:25:21.690
And what we do is we
progressively denoise in order

01:25:21.690 --> 01:25:26.890
to obtain our final output.

01:25:26.890 --> 01:25:29.650
Why wouldn't we do that?

01:25:29.650 --> 01:25:33.930
So, by the way, it
sounds very non-natural

01:25:33.930 --> 01:25:38.050
because I think you're
very used to saying things

01:25:38.050 --> 01:25:39.190
in a sequential way.

01:25:39.190 --> 01:25:44.170
But one analogy I
want to point out here

01:25:44.170 --> 01:25:47.630
is let's suppose you're
writing a speech.

01:25:49.767 --> 01:25:52.350
So first of all, I'm not sure
if you've ever written a speech.

01:25:52.350 --> 01:25:53.970
But if you were
to write a speech,

01:25:53.970 --> 01:25:56.950
you typically don't write
things in a very sequential way.

01:25:56.950 --> 01:25:59.650
You first start with a draft.

01:25:59.650 --> 01:26:01.970
You have some skeleton
of the bullet points

01:26:01.970 --> 01:26:03.730
you want to talk about.

01:26:03.730 --> 01:26:05.690
And then what you
do is you refine--

01:26:05.690 --> 01:26:08.730
go from coarse to fine grained.

01:26:08.730 --> 01:26:12.010
So what I'm saying is this
idea of doing everything

01:26:12.010 --> 01:26:17.080
at once, something that
starts from noise up to clean,

01:26:17.080 --> 01:26:22.160
although it's not natural, it
is not completely unreasonable

01:26:22.160 --> 01:26:24.520
either.

01:26:24.520 --> 01:26:27.480
Do you agree with
what I'm saying?

01:26:27.480 --> 01:26:28.400
Yeah?

01:26:28.400 --> 01:26:32.840
So let's suppose if we do
that, then what we would do

01:26:32.840 --> 01:26:40.580
is reduce the complexity from
having the number of iterations

01:26:40.580 --> 01:26:45.960
being in awe of number of
output tokens to being in awe

01:26:45.960 --> 01:26:50.560
of the steps of diffusion.

01:26:50.560 --> 01:26:53.540
So let's see how
we want to do that.

01:26:53.540 --> 01:26:57.060
So let's assume we take
text with random noise.

01:26:57.060 --> 01:26:59.840
And by the way, I'm not
defining noise just yet,

01:26:59.840 --> 01:27:02.880
but just assume that the
text is noise in a way

01:27:02.880 --> 01:27:04.800
that we'll see later.

01:27:04.800 --> 01:27:07.823
Let's suppose we have
a diffusion-based LLM.

01:27:07.823 --> 01:27:09.240
So what we're
saying is that we're

01:27:09.240 --> 01:27:16.030
having this text with less
and less noise up until having

01:27:16.030 --> 01:27:19.910
the cleaned text exactly
in the same way we

01:27:19.910 --> 01:27:22.830
have done for images.

01:27:22.830 --> 01:27:26.630
So I just want to make sure
this analogy is very clear.

01:27:26.630 --> 01:27:33.190
Here, we're denoising the whole
input text in the same way

01:27:33.190 --> 01:27:36.870
that we're denoising the
whole image all at once.

01:27:36.870 --> 01:27:40.350
Well, a natural
question is we have

01:27:40.350 --> 01:27:43.790
seen that we can think of
noise in the image world

01:27:43.790 --> 01:27:46.950
as Gaussian noise.

01:27:46.950 --> 01:27:50.510
But for text, it's a
little bit trickier.

01:27:50.510 --> 01:27:52.870
And here's the reason.

01:27:52.870 --> 01:27:56.630
In the text world,
things are discrete.

01:27:56.630 --> 01:27:57.610
So you have words.

01:27:57.610 --> 01:27:58.287
You have tokens.

01:27:58.287 --> 01:27:58.870
You have this.

01:27:58.870 --> 01:27:59.490
You have that.

01:27:59.490 --> 01:28:02.390
You're not in a
continuous world.

01:28:02.390 --> 01:28:07.150
So in the image world, you
would represent your noise

01:28:07.150 --> 01:28:10.070
with a continuous multivariate
variable, typically

01:28:10.070 --> 01:28:11.050
Gaussian noise.

01:28:11.050 --> 01:28:14.390
But how would you do with text?

01:28:14.390 --> 01:28:19.150
So one idea you can
have is let's just

01:28:19.150 --> 01:28:21.550
throw whatever token we have.

01:28:21.550 --> 01:28:23.830
That could be one option.

01:28:23.830 --> 01:28:29.870
Well, the problem is each
token has a semantic meaning.

01:28:29.870 --> 01:28:34.710
And so if you just take any
token and you put it as a way

01:28:34.710 --> 01:28:40.510
to noise your
input, then you may

01:28:40.510 --> 01:28:45.750
make your input
have a meaning that

01:28:45.750 --> 01:28:51.230
is not a meaning you may
want your input to have.

01:28:51.230 --> 01:28:56.150
So for that reason, one
thing that the field

01:28:56.150 --> 01:29:00.230
is starting to align on, which
I believe is most common thing

01:29:00.230 --> 01:29:04.510
is to have an actual
dedicated token, typically

01:29:04.510 --> 01:29:10.870
a masked token that would just
represent a text token that you

01:29:10.870 --> 01:29:12.000
would not know of.

01:29:12.000 --> 01:29:15.540
So a noised text token.

01:29:15.540 --> 01:29:18.500
So let's assume you do that.

01:29:18.500 --> 01:29:22.580
Then how would we apply
what we have seen so far?

01:29:22.580 --> 01:29:26.780
Well, we have seen
that for training--

01:29:26.780 --> 01:29:30.900
so I will spare you all the
mathematical derivations which

01:29:30.900 --> 01:29:32.320
some of these
papers they go into.

01:29:32.320 --> 01:29:34.340
We will not go into
that right now.

01:29:34.340 --> 01:29:38.180
But let's assume that we want
to mimic the training that we

01:29:38.180 --> 01:29:40.340
have done for images.

01:29:40.340 --> 01:29:42.700
How would we do that?

01:29:42.700 --> 01:29:45.820
Well, we would take
a clean sentence,

01:29:45.820 --> 01:29:48.780
as in an unmasked sentence.

01:29:48.780 --> 01:29:53.260
And what we would do
is to corrupt the text

01:29:53.260 --> 01:29:55.820
according to a noise level.

01:29:55.820 --> 01:29:57.820
And so here, what
does that mean?

01:29:57.820 --> 01:30:02.760
That means that we would have,
let's say, a t percent chance.

01:30:02.760 --> 01:30:06.180
Let's suppose t equals 0.5--
a 50% chance of a token

01:30:06.180 --> 01:30:08.740
in that sentence being masked.

01:30:08.740 --> 01:30:12.930
So what we're saying is
let's suppose t equal to 0.5.

01:30:12.930 --> 01:30:18.410
We're masking 50% of the tokens.

01:30:18.410 --> 01:30:22.850
And then our
training strategy can

01:30:22.850 --> 01:30:27.930
be to reconstruct
the tokens that

01:30:27.930 --> 01:30:33.250
were masked as a function of
whatever was in the input that

01:30:33.250 --> 01:30:35.530
was not masked.

01:30:35.530 --> 01:30:39.650
That can be your
training objective.

01:30:39.650 --> 01:30:43.050
So I know this is
not a text class,

01:30:43.050 --> 01:30:47.810
but for those of you who
know about Bert, who's

01:30:47.810 --> 01:30:51.450
an encoder-only
architecture, it also

01:30:51.450 --> 01:30:54.870
has something that is similar
as a pre-training task.

01:30:54.870 --> 01:31:00.290
The only difference is
here, the masking scheme

01:31:00.290 --> 01:31:04.030
is done with respect to a
noise level that can change,

01:31:04.030 --> 01:31:05.450
that can vary.

01:31:05.450 --> 01:31:08.110
So you can have t,
like a lot of noise.

01:31:08.110 --> 01:31:10.490
So almost everything
masked versus a little bit

01:31:10.490 --> 01:31:14.650
masked, whereas for Bert,
it's always a certain amount

01:31:14.650 --> 01:31:16.890
of masked token.

01:31:16.890 --> 01:31:19.330
I just want to call that out.

01:31:19.330 --> 01:31:21.570
So let's suppose you have that.

01:31:21.570 --> 01:31:26.370
Then what you're doing is
you're teaching your model

01:31:26.370 --> 01:31:27.590
to fill the blanks.

01:31:30.290 --> 01:31:35.010
Well, now how would you go about
actually using such a model

01:31:35.010 --> 01:31:36.530
at inference time?

01:31:36.530 --> 01:31:41.250
So what you would do is same
as what we did for images.

01:31:41.250 --> 01:31:44.190
Start with completely
noised inputs.

01:31:44.190 --> 01:31:48.170
So here a sequence
full of masked token.

01:31:48.170 --> 01:31:51.410
And then what you
would do is just

01:31:51.410 --> 01:31:56.290
predict what would be hiding
behind those masked tokens

01:31:56.290 --> 01:31:58.570
all at once.

01:31:58.570 --> 01:32:04.210
But then you want to make sure
that you have enough buffer

01:32:04.210 --> 01:32:06.440
for you to make corrections.

01:32:06.440 --> 01:32:13.080
So you may want to revise some
tokens that you have predicted.

01:32:13.080 --> 01:32:16.960
So the field has several
methods to do that, one of which

01:32:16.960 --> 01:32:22.360
is just remasking some
tokens in a random way.

01:32:22.360 --> 01:32:26.360
So some other methods, they're
mask tokens with respect

01:32:26.360 --> 01:32:27.700
to some confidence scores.

01:32:27.700 --> 01:32:29.680
For instance, if
you have a token

01:32:29.680 --> 01:32:33.840
that you're unmasking that
you're not very sure of, then

01:32:33.840 --> 01:32:37.600
you may want to
just do another try.

01:32:37.600 --> 01:32:40.960
So here, you would take some
tokens that, let's say, you're

01:32:40.960 --> 01:32:45.120
not sure of and remask them.

01:32:45.120 --> 01:32:50.400
And what you would do is
to take that new sequence

01:32:50.400 --> 01:32:54.560
and perform the same
operation, meaning

01:32:54.560 --> 01:32:58.800
trying to see who is
behind that masked token.

01:32:58.800 --> 01:33:02.120
And you do that again
and again until obtaining

01:33:02.120 --> 01:33:05.910
your final outputs.

01:33:05.910 --> 01:33:12.070
So this is a very high-level
way of how such a paradigm would

01:33:12.070 --> 01:33:14.630
work for text.

01:33:14.630 --> 01:33:20.390
Well, the good thing is there
is very active research on it.

01:33:20.390 --> 01:33:24.130
And so I was reading
one of the papers.

01:33:24.130 --> 01:33:28.550
So you can have speeds up to
10x compared to the traditional

01:33:28.550 --> 01:33:31.630
autoregressive way
of doing things.

01:33:31.630 --> 01:33:35.110
And that is particularly
useful for tasks

01:33:35.110 --> 01:33:41.150
that requires you to have
response as quick as possible.

01:33:41.150 --> 01:33:46.670
I know a lot of us are using
some form of coding agents to do

01:33:46.670 --> 01:33:48.870
our coding work these days.

01:33:48.870 --> 01:33:52.230
And I'm sure you
also want your agent

01:33:52.230 --> 01:33:55.110
to finish as
quickly as possible.

01:33:55.110 --> 01:33:58.550
Well, this paradigm would
allow you to speed up

01:33:58.550 --> 01:34:00.870
that process significantly.

01:34:00.870 --> 01:34:05.900
And that is the reason why
coding is a very good use

01:34:05.900 --> 01:34:09.060
case for that paradigm.

01:34:09.060 --> 01:34:12.820
So on the one hand
for the speed,

01:34:12.820 --> 01:34:17.780
but on the second hand
also because in coding,

01:34:17.780 --> 01:34:23.980
you naturally have a way
to be interested in filling

01:34:23.980 --> 01:34:26.060
in the middle kind of tasks.

01:34:26.060 --> 01:34:27.740
Because if you look
at your code base,

01:34:27.740 --> 01:34:31.620
you're rarely always
trying to generate

01:34:31.620 --> 01:34:35.380
things in a sequential way,
in an autoregressive way.

01:34:35.380 --> 01:34:37.780
Sometimes you have
a bunch of code

01:34:37.780 --> 01:34:39.880
and you just want
to generate code,

01:34:39.880 --> 01:34:43.900
let's say, in the
middle of two functions.

01:34:43.900 --> 01:34:47.460
So for filling the middle
tasks, that paradigm

01:34:47.460 --> 01:34:52.060
is actually better compared
to autoregressive tasks.

01:34:52.060 --> 01:34:55.000
And we will not have
time to go into details,

01:34:55.000 --> 01:34:58.580
but training is
much more expensive,

01:34:58.580 --> 01:35:03.340
particularly because
in the traditional way,

01:35:03.340 --> 01:35:08.620
the way training works is
you input your whole input

01:35:08.620 --> 01:35:14.100
and then what you're doing
is you're training your model

01:35:14.100 --> 01:35:16.640
to predict the next token.

01:35:16.640 --> 01:35:19.315
And you can parallelize
that very well.

01:35:19.315 --> 01:35:20.940
And this is unfortunately
not something

01:35:20.940 --> 01:35:25.152
that you can do as well
with the diffusion scheme.

01:35:25.152 --> 01:35:26.860
And then the second
thing is there are so

01:35:26.860 --> 01:35:31.100
many techniques that
now are specific to how

01:35:31.100 --> 01:35:35.140
autoregressive models work
that you would need to adapt.

01:35:35.140 --> 01:35:41.020
And there are also ways to
combine the benefits of the two

01:35:41.020 --> 01:35:42.660
approaches.

01:35:42.660 --> 01:35:46.900
And for instance, block
diffusion is an example of it.

01:35:46.900 --> 01:35:50.340
So I just wanted to leave you
with these screenshots that just

01:35:50.340 --> 01:35:52.700
tells you of the many
headlines that you

01:35:52.700 --> 01:35:56.380
see around companies
that are betting

01:35:56.380 --> 01:35:57.840
on this kind of technology.

01:35:57.840 --> 01:36:01.970
So you have one such
company called Inception

01:36:01.970 --> 01:36:05.650
that's actually founded
by a professor at Stanford

01:36:05.650 --> 01:36:07.890
that's doing great.

01:36:07.890 --> 01:36:12.490
And I will just leave you with
some suggested readings in case

01:36:12.490 --> 01:36:13.230
you want.

01:36:13.230 --> 01:36:14.710
And yeah.

01:36:14.710 --> 01:36:15.710
That's a great question.

01:36:15.710 --> 01:36:19.290
So the question is, how do
you handle the variable output

01:36:19.290 --> 01:36:20.010
length?

01:36:20.010 --> 01:36:21.410
It's a great question.

01:36:21.410 --> 01:36:23.690
There are a few ways
you can deal with that.

01:36:23.690 --> 01:36:28.330
But in practice, you
indeed need to set

01:36:28.330 --> 01:36:30.410
a given length for your output.

01:36:30.410 --> 01:36:33.810
And what you do is
you stop whatever

01:36:33.810 --> 01:36:38.610
is after a special token
called end of sentence token.

01:36:38.610 --> 01:36:43.290
Now, you can imagine
cases where you're

01:36:43.290 --> 01:36:45.530
expecting a way bigger
output, but you always

01:36:45.530 --> 01:36:46.550
have shorter outputs.

01:36:46.550 --> 01:36:48.850
So you're wasting a
lot of computations.

01:36:48.850 --> 01:36:51.730
So that's why the last
method I mentioned

01:36:51.730 --> 01:36:53.470
is something that can be useful.

01:36:53.470 --> 01:36:57.000
So block diffusion
is one approach

01:36:57.000 --> 01:37:00.080
that's being worked on
where you're actually

01:37:00.080 --> 01:37:03.240
generating text block by block.

01:37:03.240 --> 01:37:05.560
So you're saying, OK, I'm
going to generate, let's say,

01:37:05.560 --> 01:37:07.420
text of size, let's say, 100.

01:37:07.420 --> 01:37:10.120
And I'm going to use
diffusion for that.

01:37:10.120 --> 01:37:13.520
And once you generate
such an output,

01:37:13.520 --> 01:37:16.760
if you didn't finish
the output, then you

01:37:16.760 --> 01:37:19.800
will use that as a
condition to perform

01:37:19.800 --> 01:37:21.900
another round of diffusion
for the second block

01:37:21.900 --> 01:37:23.840
and so on and so
forth until hitting

01:37:23.840 --> 01:37:26.040
an end of sentence token.

01:37:26.040 --> 01:37:27.680
So you touch a very
good point, which

01:37:27.680 --> 01:37:31.360
is there are some differences
between text and image.

01:37:31.360 --> 01:37:35.000
And the variable
length is one of them.

01:37:35.000 --> 01:37:37.140
Yep.

01:37:37.140 --> 01:37:37.640
Yeah.

01:37:37.640 --> 01:37:38.800
[INAUDIBLE]

01:37:38.800 --> 01:37:41.480
So the question is,
can you apply this

01:37:41.480 --> 01:37:43.700
to other discrete
things other than text?

01:37:43.700 --> 01:37:45.760
I'm sure yes.

01:37:45.760 --> 01:37:50.520
We will not cover this
today, but the ways people

01:37:50.520 --> 01:37:53.240
have derived a tractable
mathematical way

01:37:53.240 --> 01:37:56.960
to do that in the text
world is something

01:37:56.960 --> 01:38:01.380
that involves transcribing
things for discrete items.

01:38:01.380 --> 01:38:04.400
So I'm sure that it can be done.

01:38:04.400 --> 01:38:07.640
So I would actually highly
recommend the first paper

01:38:07.640 --> 01:38:09.560
for you to get a
sense of how people

01:38:09.560 --> 01:38:12.680
go from continuous to discrete.

01:38:12.680 --> 01:38:14.560
Yeah.

01:38:14.560 --> 01:38:15.540
Yeah, great question.

01:38:15.540 --> 01:38:17.440
So the question is,
can you not consider

01:38:17.440 --> 01:38:20.080
text as just being a
screenshot of-- sorry,

01:38:20.080 --> 01:38:23.280
can you not consider text
as being images of text

01:38:23.280 --> 01:38:26.907
and use some kind of OCR
mechanism to process that?

01:38:26.907 --> 01:38:28.740
So I think it's definitely
a great approach.

01:38:28.740 --> 01:38:31.000
There's actually
a paper that was

01:38:31.000 --> 01:38:33.480
published a few months
ago, DeepSeek OCR, that

01:38:33.480 --> 01:38:34.840
goes into that.

01:38:34.840 --> 01:38:36.380
I do think it's promising.

01:38:36.380 --> 01:38:40.560
I believe there is some savings
with respect to how many tokens

01:38:40.560 --> 01:38:44.320
that fills compared to how many
tokens the actual text would

01:38:44.320 --> 01:38:45.200
fill in.

01:38:45.200 --> 01:38:47.320
So it's definitely
another directions

01:38:47.320 --> 01:38:48.540
that I think is promising.

01:38:51.600 --> 01:38:52.670
Cool.

01:38:52.670 --> 01:38:56.230
With that, I'll
give it to Shervine.

01:38:56.230 --> 01:38:57.910
Thank you, Afshine.

01:38:57.910 --> 01:39:01.970
So welcome to the last
stretch of CME 96.

01:39:01.970 --> 01:39:04.030
So we're going to see
together some closing

01:39:04.030 --> 01:39:07.350
thoughts on what
lies ahead, as well

01:39:07.350 --> 01:39:10.230
as some challenges that we see.

01:39:10.230 --> 01:39:12.470
So first, let's look at today.

01:39:12.470 --> 01:39:15.470
So when you look at
existing top models,

01:39:15.470 --> 01:39:19.710
what is the typical price you
have to pay for a perfect image?

01:39:19.710 --> 01:39:21.490
So I did the research for you.

01:39:21.490 --> 01:39:24.410
I looked at major
labs across the board,

01:39:24.410 --> 01:39:30.030
and it seems that the price per
megapixel is of about $0.01.

01:39:30.030 --> 01:39:33.590
And I think that's going to
be a useful metric to track

01:39:33.590 --> 01:39:37.790
over time to see the
transition from this being

01:39:37.790 --> 01:39:41.950
a nice thing to a commodity.

01:39:41.950 --> 01:39:45.670
And of course, in reality, you
wouldn't use these top models

01:39:45.670 --> 01:39:48.790
that I listed here, but
more of a distilled version.

01:39:48.790 --> 01:39:52.420
But still, the upper
bound gives a good view

01:39:52.420 --> 01:39:58.620
of what people would be ready
to pay for perfect quality.

01:39:58.620 --> 01:40:03.260
But then let's look at the
kinds of challenges that could

01:40:03.260 --> 01:40:05.980
be tackled in the near term.

01:40:05.980 --> 01:40:09.660
So one thing that you
notice is that images,

01:40:09.660 --> 01:40:12.580
they are doing
well on themselves.

01:40:12.580 --> 01:40:14.080
But there are wins.

01:40:14.080 --> 01:40:18.220
In other modalities that you
are inclined to include in order

01:40:18.220 --> 01:40:20.940
to make generation
more powerful.

01:40:20.940 --> 01:40:25.880
So one example that I have in
mind is reasoning with images.

01:40:25.880 --> 01:40:30.340
So right now, anecdotally,
if you ask for a diagram,

01:40:30.340 --> 01:40:34.340
you might get, with most
models, a projection

01:40:34.340 --> 01:40:38.300
of what you asked on an
image, which is a nice start.

01:40:38.300 --> 01:40:41.500
But it's not quite to the
same level of refinement

01:40:41.500 --> 01:40:44.420
as the one you might get
in the text world, where

01:40:44.420 --> 01:40:48.100
you ask a very precise
question and the model

01:40:48.100 --> 01:40:51.890
goes in searching for
a meaning behind it

01:40:51.890 --> 01:40:55.850
and then projecting the exact
answer in a concise way.

01:40:55.850 --> 01:41:01.250
So that seems like a
good area of research.

01:41:01.250 --> 01:41:04.370
And then one topic that
I've seen mentioned here.

01:41:04.370 --> 01:41:07.290
So for image editing
use cases, it

01:41:07.290 --> 01:41:10.830
seems that we're being
too hard on ourselves.

01:41:10.830 --> 01:41:14.650
We're doing the whole generation
process that is, by nature,

01:41:14.650 --> 01:41:15.810
unconstrained.

01:41:15.810 --> 01:41:18.210
And it seems we
have an opportunity

01:41:18.210 --> 01:41:22.650
to use existing tools and
wins in the text world,

01:41:22.650 --> 01:41:28.090
to use agents and
existing human expertise

01:41:28.090 --> 01:41:35.130
to make editing happen in a
constrained and tractable way.

01:41:35.130 --> 01:41:38.530
And then you have
other use cases

01:41:38.530 --> 01:41:41.410
that could seem
attractive to you all.

01:41:41.410 --> 01:41:43.910
For example, learning
about a class,

01:41:43.910 --> 01:41:45.770
you have several
streams of information

01:41:45.770 --> 01:41:48.970
that come from texts, for
example, the slides, the lecture

01:41:48.970 --> 01:41:53.190
video, and also the audio track.

01:41:53.190 --> 01:41:58.788
So while you can put pieces
together one by one today,

01:41:58.788 --> 01:42:00.330
it seems that there
is an opportunity

01:42:00.330 --> 01:42:04.930
to bring all modalities to a
consistent and coherent way

01:42:04.930 --> 01:42:10.450
and generate
synthesis out of this.

01:42:10.450 --> 01:42:14.850
And in the longer run, you
can see the consequences

01:42:14.850 --> 01:42:17.530
of all these benefits
across industries

01:42:17.530 --> 01:42:21.050
that might be lagging because
of some inertia factor.

01:42:21.050 --> 01:42:24.150
So you can think of so
the field of robotics,

01:42:24.150 --> 01:42:27.330
which seems to be the
next area that could

01:42:27.330 --> 01:42:28.830
benefit from a revolution.

01:42:28.830 --> 01:42:33.750
So in text, we can generate text
now like roughly solved images.

01:42:33.750 --> 01:42:35.930
Now we see great
images roughly solved.

01:42:35.930 --> 01:42:37.570
And it seems we
have an opportunity

01:42:37.570 --> 01:42:42.050
to feed all these learnings
into the ingredients that

01:42:42.050 --> 01:42:47.240
could make robotics pick up, for
example, environments, but also

01:42:47.240 --> 01:42:51.720
medicine and other
desk jobs that

01:42:51.720 --> 01:42:54.680
might need their own
process of approvals

01:42:54.680 --> 01:42:57.920
to actually
incorporate these wins.

01:42:57.920 --> 01:43:02.600
And maybe one day you won't
need to come to lecture at all,

01:43:02.600 --> 01:43:06.840
and there will be some model
gathering all the pieces for you

01:43:06.840 --> 01:43:10.680
in a coherent and pedagogic way.

01:43:10.680 --> 01:43:13.280
But I think we
might still be far

01:43:13.280 --> 01:43:17.880
from that, because the fact of
transmitting knowledge still

01:43:17.880 --> 01:43:21.920
requires that taste and opinion
that even the text world doesn't

01:43:21.920 --> 01:43:22.420
have.

01:43:22.420 --> 01:43:26.360
So I'm quite curious to
see when that will happen.

01:43:26.360 --> 01:43:30.120
On the challenges side, so as
we have seen, there are costs.

01:43:30.120 --> 01:43:34.420
And you can use distillation
to alleviate a bit of that.

01:43:34.420 --> 01:43:37.160
But also, there is research
on the hardware side

01:43:37.160 --> 01:43:40.640
where right now
hardware is built

01:43:40.640 --> 01:43:42.500
on top of matrix multiplies.

01:43:42.500 --> 01:43:46.110
But as you have seen,
the building block

01:43:46.110 --> 01:43:50.150
of these days models is
transformers and which relies

01:43:50.150 --> 01:43:51.990
on the concept of attention.

01:43:51.990 --> 01:43:55.030
And this attention has a
very precise succession

01:43:55.030 --> 01:43:58.710
of operations that you
could think of simplifying

01:43:58.710 --> 01:44:01.170
in an analog to numeric way.

01:44:01.170 --> 01:44:05.390
So this research paper
could be one such pointer.

01:44:05.390 --> 01:44:08.610
But also one other concern
is the data quality side

01:44:08.610 --> 01:44:11.990
because today, you
see all these images

01:44:11.990 --> 01:44:14.930
that are being generated
that are out in the wild.

01:44:14.930 --> 01:44:18.790
So for the next
generation of models,

01:44:18.790 --> 01:44:21.110
you might encounter
the issue of not

01:44:21.110 --> 01:44:25.550
finding the true distribution p
data, as we had assumed we had.

01:44:25.550 --> 01:44:28.950
And you have some
papers that study

01:44:28.950 --> 01:44:32.590
the phenomenon of so-called
model collapse, where

01:44:32.590 --> 01:44:38.590
feeding what has been generated
is a way for [AUDIO OUT].

01:44:38.590 --> 01:44:43.230
So it looks like an echo chamber
of mistakes that keep growing.

01:44:43.230 --> 01:44:46.230
And you see that at the end,
if you represent embeddings

01:44:46.230 --> 01:44:51.470
of generations somewhere, you
could clearly cluster the two.

01:44:51.470 --> 01:44:54.270
But how could we deal with that?

01:44:54.270 --> 01:44:57.550
So that's the kind
of trust point

01:44:57.550 --> 01:44:59.050
which is key in our society.

01:44:59.050 --> 01:45:01.970
You want to be able
to trust images.

01:45:01.970 --> 01:45:05.550
And now these days,
with how similar

01:45:05.550 --> 01:45:08.690
the true data distribution
and generated images are,

01:45:08.690 --> 01:45:11.950
it seems like the
border is becoming thin.

01:45:11.950 --> 01:45:14.110
So two ways to counter that.

01:45:14.110 --> 01:45:20.310
There is a norm called C2PA
that has been gaining traction

01:45:20.310 --> 01:45:22.050
across software companies.

01:45:22.050 --> 01:45:25.630
So you might see this
AI info popping in.

01:45:25.630 --> 01:45:28.350
It's because images
that you create with AI

01:45:28.350 --> 01:45:32.770
have some of history attached
to it that surfaces it.

01:45:32.770 --> 01:45:36.470
So that could be one way to
tackle the data collection

01:45:36.470 --> 01:45:39.420
quality issue that
I mentioned before.

01:45:39.420 --> 01:45:43.740
But the thing is, if you
take a screenshot of this,

01:45:43.740 --> 01:45:46.360
the concept of
metadata disappears.

01:45:46.360 --> 01:45:47.820
So are we doomed?

01:45:47.820 --> 01:45:48.980
Actually, no.

01:45:48.980 --> 01:45:54.200
There are other ways that we
could employ to get around this.

01:45:54.200 --> 01:45:58.860
One example is watermarking
with the example of SynthID

01:45:58.860 --> 01:46:04.500
from Google DeepMind that
hides behind pixels patterns

01:46:04.500 --> 01:46:07.900
that reveal its origin.

01:46:07.900 --> 01:46:09.820
So beyond that, you
also have the topic

01:46:09.820 --> 01:46:12.100
of safety, which
is very important.

01:46:12.100 --> 01:46:16.740
And creating images that
are harmful to others

01:46:16.740 --> 01:46:19.640
can have societal implications.

01:46:19.640 --> 01:46:23.640
So there is two
parts to fixing this.

01:46:23.640 --> 01:46:26.460
One is on the model
side where each company

01:46:26.460 --> 01:46:30.800
has their own policies to
guard such generations.

01:46:30.800 --> 01:46:34.020
But also law is picking up.

01:46:34.020 --> 01:46:37.400
And what can you do from there?

01:46:37.400 --> 01:46:39.290
So let's say, this class ends.

01:46:39.290 --> 01:46:41.570
How can you be updated?

01:46:41.570 --> 01:46:48.290
So one suggestion is to take
a look at the relevant archive

01:46:48.290 --> 01:46:50.630
section on computer vision.

01:46:50.630 --> 01:46:54.050
But you might see that the
number of submissions every day

01:46:54.050 --> 01:46:55.570
is of 100.

01:46:55.570 --> 01:46:58.050
So this might not be tractable.

01:46:58.050 --> 01:47:00.930
But on the other
hand, some venues

01:47:00.930 --> 01:47:03.670
do a good job at distilling
some of these works.

01:47:03.670 --> 01:47:05.770
Not all, but they
can give you an idea

01:47:05.770 --> 01:47:08.930
of where the field is heading.

01:47:08.930 --> 01:47:13.550
And something else that I want
to tell you is all these papers,

01:47:13.550 --> 01:47:16.610
we saw a bunch of formulas
all over the place.

01:47:16.610 --> 01:47:19.210
And sometimes you just
want to know how they work.

01:47:19.210 --> 01:47:25.130
And a great pattern that we see
in academia and in the industry

01:47:25.130 --> 01:47:28.410
is that there is code
release of what was

01:47:28.410 --> 01:47:30.810
used to design a given method.

01:47:30.810 --> 01:47:34.290
So I highly encourage
cloning the GitHub repo

01:47:34.290 --> 01:47:37.680
and playing with an AI
assistant coding software

01:47:37.680 --> 01:47:40.180
on just getting you
through the flow.

01:47:40.180 --> 01:47:43.360
And you will learn a
ton just by doing that.

01:47:43.360 --> 01:47:46.380
And beyond that, you
have other resources.

01:47:46.380 --> 01:47:51.480
So on Twitter, you have a
growing community of people

01:47:51.480 --> 01:47:52.820
who talk about these topics.

01:47:52.820 --> 01:47:56.240
So if your For
You page turns out

01:47:56.240 --> 01:47:59.180
to be in the clusters
of these topics,

01:47:59.180 --> 01:48:02.280
you can get a lot of
interesting recommendations.

01:48:02.280 --> 01:48:06.680
And another highlight is other
Stanford courses that talk about

01:48:06.680 --> 01:48:11.400
vision, like 231n
that I recommend.

01:48:11.400 --> 01:48:13.880
And there is this
study guide that we

01:48:13.880 --> 01:48:16.000
shared at the
beginning of the class

01:48:16.000 --> 01:48:22.400
that we aim at keeping up
to date across the years.

01:48:22.400 --> 01:48:25.080
But beyond all
that, Afshine and I

01:48:25.080 --> 01:48:28.680
wanted to say that we are very
happy to come here every Friday

01:48:28.680 --> 01:48:31.240
and teach this class
and spend time with you.

01:48:31.240 --> 01:48:33.280
It has been a pleasure for us.

01:48:33.280 --> 01:48:39.100
And thank you, hidden heroes,
who came here on the evenings

01:48:39.100 --> 01:48:41.560
and asked so many
great questions.

01:48:41.560 --> 01:48:45.160
You were one of the reasons
why the class was so dynamic

01:48:45.160 --> 01:48:47.800
and generated insights
that could then

01:48:47.800 --> 01:48:50.560
be useful to everyone.

01:48:50.560 --> 01:48:53.438
Thank you also to
those watching online.

01:48:53.438 --> 01:48:55.480
Thank you for the great
questions on Ed.

01:48:55.480 --> 01:48:59.760
And I hope the class
was of value to you.

01:48:59.760 --> 01:49:03.400
And if you are
around in the fall,

01:49:03.400 --> 01:49:06.460
Afshine and I will be
teaching a similar class,

01:49:06.460 --> 01:49:10.460
but in the text world where
it will be our second edition.

01:49:10.460 --> 01:49:13.100
And if you are in
for a new ride,

01:49:13.100 --> 01:49:16.840
then we'd love to see you again.

01:49:16.840 --> 01:49:21.760
And our favorite teddy bear
is coming one last time

01:49:21.760 --> 01:49:26.040
to wish you a great
summer and all the best.

01:49:26.040 --> 01:49:26.580
Thank you.

01:49:26.580 --> 01:49:29.930
[APPLAUSE]

